[{:db/id 17592186176044 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cbcdbef5-9dc5-5670-8a90-267993182080"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-cbcdbef5-9dc5-5670-8a90-267993182080" :question/id #uuid "cbcdbef5-9dc5-5670-8a90-267993182080" :question/math-academy-id "q-166838" :question/problem "![](images/04/04676bef8cc37d05e404dc03211968ebaf39f7adbfc54a4a2cb4d5f8cdc3f1a9.png)\n\nA circular cake is cut into three slices, as shown above. Given that $m∠A=23z+{4}^{∘},$ $m∠B=12z+{6}^{∘},$ and $m∠C=15z,$ then$\\,z=$\n{{field-1}}\n${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the entire cake corresponds to the full angle of ${360}^{∘}.$\n\nBy the angle addition postulate,\n\n$$\nm∠A+m∠B+m∠C={360}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by substituting the expressions for $m∠A,$ $m∠B,$ and $m∠C,$ and solving for $z,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ (23z+{4}^{∘})+(12z+{6}^{∘})+(15z) & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 50z+{10}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 50z & ={350}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={7}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6482d0d5-ec8d-54c5-8c10-350eede2f3ff"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6482d0d5-ec8d-54c5-8c10-350eede2f3ff" :answer-field/id #uuid "6482d0d5-ec8d-54c5-8c10-350eede2f3ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-25891548-1783-5b6a-b98e-416aa7ad7ec4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-25891548-1783-5b6a-b98e-416aa7ad7ec4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25891548-1783-5b6a-b98e-416aa7ad7ec4" :answer/id #uuid "25891548-1783-5b6a-b98e-416aa7ad7ec4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110761 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-16a5b9ce-8ad5-5f67-b7e3-0fec61fbfd8f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-16a5b9ce-8ad5-5f67-b7e3-0fec61fbfd8f" :question/id #uuid "16a5b9ce-8ad5-5f67-b7e3-0fec61fbfd8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-166839" :question/problem "Which slice ($A,$ $B,$ or $C$) of the cake slices from part 1 corresponds to a right angle?\n\nSlice$\\,$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In part 1, we found that $z={7}^{∘}.$ \n\nWe substitute it into the expression for angles and obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =23({7}^{∘})+{4}^{∘}={165}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & =12({7}^{∘})+{6}^{∘}={90}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & =15({7}^{∘})={105}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, slice $B$ corresponds to a right angle." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b6295df7-9006-5cdd-b997-240187450a04"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b6295df7-9006-5cdd-b997-240187450a04" :answer-field/id #uuid "b6295df7-9006-5cdd-b997-240187450a04" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f9cae770-6109-54d3-b8a4-8df741927514" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f9cae770-6109-54d3-b8a4-8df741927514"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9cae770-6109-54d3-b8a4-8df741927514" :answer/id #uuid "f9cae770-6109-54d3-b8a4-8df741927514" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "B" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110619 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-67306212-e963-567c-a3f1-8493d029556a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-67306212-e963-567c-a3f1-8493d029556a" :question/id #uuid "67306212-e963-567c-a3f1-8493d029556a" :question/math-academy-id "q-166840" :question/problem "How many cakes can the pastry shop produce with $9$ packs of chocolate?\n\nThe pastry shop can produce$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$cakes." :question/worked-solution "To produce $x$ cakes, the pastry shop uses $(\\frac{5x+3}{2})$ packs of chocolate. So, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{5x+3}{2}=9.\n$$\n\nWe need to solve this linear equation for $x.$\nFirst, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x+3}{2} & =\\frac{9}{1} \\\\ (5x+3)\\cdot 1 & =9\\cdot 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5x+3)\\cdot 1 & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\ 5x+3 & =18 \\\\ 5x+3-3 & =18-3 \\\\ 5x & =15 \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5} & =\\frac{15}{5} \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the pastry shop can produce $3$ cakes." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2216ef52-715e-5e56-a65f-58cd2ceb2ca9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2216ef52-715e-5e56-a65f-58cd2ceb2ca9" :answer-field/id #uuid "2216ef52-715e-5e56-a65f-58cd2ceb2ca9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2c1738da-f777-5f76-9b75-c6e7cc26ee58" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2c1738da-f777-5f76-9b75-c6e7cc26ee58"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c1738da-f777-5f76-9b75-c6e7cc26ee58" :answer/id #uuid "2c1738da-f777-5f76-9b75-c6e7cc26ee58" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186110620 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b477291d-9f4b-5add-8cfc-437d184095c2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b477291d-9f4b-5add-8cfc-437d184095c2" :question/id #uuid "b477291d-9f4b-5add-8cfc-437d184095c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-166841" :question/problem "How many cakes does the pastry shop produce if they use equal numbers of packs of flour and butter?\n\nThe pastry shop produces$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$cakes." :question/worked-solution "To produce $x$ cakes, the pastry shop uses the following amounts:\n\n* $\\frac{4x+1}{6}$ packs of butter\n\n* $\\frac{13x+5}{20}$ packs of flour\n\nIf the pastry shop uses the same number of packs of butter and flour, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{4x+1}{6}=\\frac{13x+5}{20}.\n$$\n\nWe need to solve this linear equation for $x.$ First, we cross-multiply:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4x+1}{6} & =\\frac{13x+5}{20} \\\\ (4x+1)\\cdot 20 & =6\\cdot (13x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we proceed as usual to solve the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4x+1)\\cdot 20 & =6\\cdot (13x+5) \\\\ 80x+20 & =78x+30 \\\\ 80x+20-78x & =78x+30-78x \\\\ 2x+20 & =30 \\\\ 2x+20-20 & =30-20 \\\\ 2x & =10 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $x=5$ cakes are produced when the pastry shop uses the same number of packs of butter and flour." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2504adaf-a330-523d-8d22-11511f8bc70d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2504adaf-a330-523d-8d22-11511f8bc70d" :answer-field/id #uuid "2504adaf-a330-523d-8d22-11511f8bc70d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d2a3a79c-7ba3-5ecb-b30e-0a41e9cc0d71" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d2a3a79c-7ba3-5ecb-b30e-0a41e9cc0d71"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2a3a79c-7ba3-5ecb-b30e-0a41e9cc0d71" :answer/id #uuid "d2a3a79c-7ba3-5ecb-b30e-0a41e9cc0d71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186073752 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ededc7bf-b310-5714-a047-930df525362b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ededc7bf-b310-5714-a047-930df525362b" :question/id #uuid "ededc7bf-b310-5714-a047-930df525362b" :question/math-academy-id "q-166843" :question/problem "The pastry shop's total cost and revenue per month (in dollars) from selling cakes can be modeled by the equations $y=1\\,600+5n$ and $y=64+17n,$ respectively, where $n$ is the number of cakes sold. \n\nSolve the system below using the substitution method to find the exact number of cakes needed to break even and the revenue made.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=1\\,600+5n \\\\ y=64+17n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pastry shop breaks even when$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$cakes are produced. \n\nThe revenue made when selling this number of cakes is$\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,$dollars." :question/worked-solution "We will isolate a variable in one of the equations and then substitute it into the other equation. \n\nNotice that $y$ is isolated in the first equation. Therefore, we can replace $y$ with $1\\,600+5n$ in the second equation and solve for $n:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =64+17n \\\\ 1\\,600+5n & =64+17n \\\\ 1\\,600+5n-64 & =64+17n-64 \\\\ 1\\,536+5n & =17n \\\\ 1\\,536+5n-5n & =17n-5n \\\\ 1\\,536 & =12n \\\\ \\frac{1\\,536}{12} & =\\frac{12n}{12} \\\\ 128 & =n \\\\ n & =128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we now substitute $n=128$ into $y=64+17n$, then we can find the value of $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=64+17(128)=2\\,240\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solution to the system of equations is $n=128$ and $y=2\\,240.$\nTherefore, the pastry shop breaks even when $128$ units are produced, and the revenue made when selling this number of units is $$2\\,240.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1c523c48-ec60-5fa0-9eec-5d07498f1571" "answer-field-fb7943f0-b18a-5a1c-9e1c-77a8a3b53234"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1c523c48-ec60-5fa0-9eec-5d07498f1571" :answer-field/id #uuid "1c523c48-ec60-5fa0-9eec-5d07498f1571" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-951ed025-e4b5-517b-830a-a3da54b9c6e7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-951ed025-e4b5-517b-830a-a3da54b9c6e7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-951ed025-e4b5-517b-830a-a3da54b9c6e7" :answer/id #uuid "951ed025-e4b5-517b-830a-a3da54b9c6e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "128" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fb7943f0-b18a-5a1c-9e1c-77a8a3b53234" :answer-field/id #uuid "fb7943f0-b18a-5a1c-9e1c-77a8a3b53234" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-eebbe5cc-91ee-5b03-9257-cc5f956ce523" :answer-field/choices ["answer-eebbe5cc-91ee-5b03-9257-cc5f956ce523"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eebbe5cc-91ee-5b03-9257-cc5f956ce523" :answer/id #uuid "eebbe5cc-91ee-5b03-9257-cc5f956ce523" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2240" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "multistep-641ef0d3-29e1-5b1f-8a4e-97ad97f061b8" :multistep/id #uuid "641ef0d3-29e1-5b1f-8a4e-97ad97f061b8" :multistep/context "To make $x$ cakes, a pastry shop uses the following amounts of ingredients:\n\n- $\\frac{5x+3}{2}$ packs of chocolate\n\n- $\\frac{4x+1}{6}$ packs of butter\n\n- $\\frac{13x+5}{20}$ packs of flour" :multistep/steps ["step-7a61747e-c38c-584e-8963-46aaeb9e15fc" "step-ef5b46fd-2c6e-5377-86f7-547954d00327" "step-c9a6b498-523d-5ae1-a73c-a7d6564a5ea4" "step-982a0197-ff18-5665-b059-933e7b15ad60" "step-8c67644a-ba6b-5056-a110-d30abc262ecb"] :multistep/first-step "step-7a61747e-c38c-584e-8963-46aaeb9e15fc" :db/ensure [:multistep/validate]} {:db/id "step-7a61747e-c38c-584e-8963-46aaeb9e15fc" :step/id #uuid "7a61747e-c38c-584e-8963-46aaeb9e15fc" :step/content "question-cbcdbef5-9dc5-5670-8a90-267993182080" :step/next "step-ef5b46fd-2c6e-5377-86f7-547954d00327" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-ef5b46fd-2c6e-5377-86f7-547954d00327" :step/id #uuid "ef5b46fd-2c6e-5377-86f7-547954d00327" :step/content "question-16a5b9ce-8ad5-5f67-b7e3-0fec61fbfd8f" :step/next "step-c9a6b498-523d-5ae1-a73c-a7d6564a5ea4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c9a6b498-523d-5ae1-a73c-a7d6564a5ea4" :step/id #uuid "c9a6b498-523d-5ae1-a73c-a7d6564a5ea4" :step/content "question-67306212-e963-567c-a3f1-8493d029556a" :step/next "step-982a0197-ff18-5665-b059-933e7b15ad60" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-982a0197-ff18-5665-b059-933e7b15ad60" :step/id #uuid "982a0197-ff18-5665-b059-933e7b15ad60" :step/content "question-b477291d-9f4b-5add-8cfc-437d184095c2" :step/next "step-8c67644a-ba6b-5056-a110-d30abc262ecb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8c67644a-ba6b-5056-a110-d30abc262ecb" :step/id #uuid "8c67644a-ba6b-5056-a110-d30abc262ecb" :step/content "question-ededc7bf-b310-5714-a047-930df525362b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "assigned-problem-d603d340-e01d-5e3d-8700-e4c91221a0f2" :assigned-problem/id #uuid "d603d340-e01d-5e3d-8700-e4c91221a0f2" :assigned-problem/content "multistep-641ef0d3-29e1-5b1f-8a4e-97ad97f061b8" :assigned-problem/topic-coverage [17592186050164 17592186048174 17592186049846 17592186048826] :db/ensure [:assigned-problem/validate]} {:db/id "activity-827fbf6e-9511-507b-adad-43d709f85170" :activity/id #uuid "827fbf6e-9511-507b-adad-43d709f85170" :activity/title "Multi-Step Linear Equations in Catering Contexts" :activity/type :activity.type/assignment :activity/steps ["step-61cb8af8-6aaa-5731-948a-249a87d1c6fc"] :activity/first-step "step-61cb8af8-6aaa-5731-948a-249a87d1c6fc" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-61cb8af8-6aaa-5731-948a-249a87d1c6fc" :step/id #uuid "61cb8af8-6aaa-5731-948a-249a87d1c6fc" :step/content "assigned-problem-d603d340-e01d-5e3d-8700-e4c91221a0f2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229161 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-74bb58bc-5799-594f-9fe1-c2f6de777ad6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229646 :question/problem "For which of the following values is the Runge-Kutta Method (RK4) stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=2$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the RK4 method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δ)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the RK4 Method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(\\lambda Δx)^{4}}{24}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the RK4 Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.4)| & =|1-0.4+\\frac{(0.4)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(0.4)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(0.4)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{419}{625}| \\\\  & =0.670\\,4<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1)| & =|1-1+\\frac{(1)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(1)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(1)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3}{8}| \\\\  & =0.375<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2)| & =|1-2+\\frac{(2)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(2)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(2)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{3}| \\\\  & =0.333…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the RK4 Method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(3)| & =|1-3+\\frac{(3)^{2}}{2}-\\frac{(3)^{3}}{6}+\\frac{(3)^{4}}{24}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{11}{8}| \\\\  & =1.375<1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *outside* the stability region, the RK4 Method is unstable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\".\n\nNote that the stability region of the RK4 Method is $0<\\lambda Δx<2.785…,$ so it is not absolutely stable." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228100 :answer-field/correct 17592186228104 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229160 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-25d64691-5b6a-542f-814d-ce1c552ea7d3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-25d64691-5b6a-542f-814d-ce1c552ea7d3" :question/id #uuid "25d64691-5b6a-542f-814d-ce1c552ea7d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-333544" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the implicit Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =4,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The stability function is $R(4Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}.$\n\nII. The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is absolutely stable.\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.1.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-4{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+4{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+4Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(4Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(4Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n$|\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}|<1.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Now, since $1+4Δx>1$ for any positive step size $Δx,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{1+4Δx}|<1\n$$\n\nfor all $Δx>0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, because the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for step size $Δx=0.1.$ Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a124cc3f-17e0-53b5-aed5-2049adc80c6e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a124cc3f-17e0-53b5-aed5-2049adc80c6e" :answer-field/id #uuid "a124cc3f-17e0-53b5-aed5-2049adc80c6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-80c052ba-48ac-56d0-8ff1-de202eea8817" :answer-field/choices ["answer-525fd160-0089-59c0-a76b-1ab57865318a" "answer-469a895b-ff19-5c22-9203-6eb75d050f16" "answer-80c052ba-48ac-56d0-8ff1-de202eea8817" "answer-501f49f4-5d2c-516d-b744-0038bbab282c" "answer-3c86d4ff-e60d-5ed0-9b7e-51fd840bb413"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-525fd160-0089-59c0-a76b-1ab57865318a" :answer/id #uuid "525fd160-0089-59c0-a76b-1ab57865318a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-469a895b-ff19-5c22-9203-6eb75d050f16" :answer/id #uuid "469a895b-ff19-5c22-9203-6eb75d050f16" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-80c052ba-48ac-56d0-8ff1-de202eea8817" :answer/id #uuid "80c052ba-48ac-56d0-8ff1-de202eea8817" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-501f49f4-5d2c-516d-b744-0038bbab282c" :answer/id #uuid "501f49f4-5d2c-516d-b744-0038bbab282c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c86d4ff-e60d-5ed0-9b7e-51fd840bb413" :answer/id #uuid "3c86d4ff-e60d-5ed0-9b7e-51fd840bb413" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-74bb58bc-5799-594f-9fe1-c2f6de777ad6" :question/id #uuid "74bb58bc-5799-594f-9fe1-c2f6de777ad6" :question/math-academy-id "q-334247" :question/problem "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1.3$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=4$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=0.05$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the Implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.4)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.4}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.4}| \\\\  & =0.7142…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.3)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.3}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.3}| \\\\  & =0.4347…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(4)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+4}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{5}| \\\\  & =0.2<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.05,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.05)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.05}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.05}| \\\\  & =0.9523<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\".\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f48506ec-17d9-5b93-9a61-0014927029a1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f48506ec-17d9-5b93-9a61-0014927029a1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f48506ec-17d9-5b93-9a61-0014927029a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d6203cbd-f862-5bd5-a4a5-da7556b09cdd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2c4e0507-df7f-5aa5-98f1-856c82ebe79f" "answer-78dba489-7ba7-5d7e-a78f-ab1fdacb56a7" "answer-d6203cbd-f862-5bd5-a4a5-da7556b09cdd" "answer-748cd873-307f-5e75-9f8f-77a3d766273d" "answer-f4d3c51b-b3a8-5879-b596-2a32d0b50eda"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c4e0507-df7f-5aa