 :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C{e}^{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d43c47fe-8dfe-5ab2-bf30-ae9c8091017a" :answer/id #uuid "d43c47fe-8dfe-5ab2-bf30-ae9c8091017a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c9fa48ae-3277-50cc-aaef-292c47d4e634" :answer-field/id #uuid "c9fa48ae-3277-50cc-aaef-292c47d4e634" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-66939b7f-8b1a-5f43-a886-d674d4e89006" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e90361c9-8b35-5852-ab40-ca1c4b040de9" "answer-07f30960-08f8-59bf-90ed-3b5763ed9e49" "answer-66939b7f-8b1a-5f43-a886-d674d4e89006"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e90361c9-8b35-5852-ab40-ca1c4b040de9" :answer/id #uuid "e90361c9-8b35-5852-ab40-ca1c4b040de9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07f30960-08f8-59bf-90ed-3b5763ed9e49" :answer/id #uuid "07f30960-08f8-59bf-90ed-3b5763ed9e49" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x\\in R$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66939b7f-8b1a-5f43-a886-d674d4e89006" :answer/id #uuid "66939b7f-8b1a-5f43-a886-d674d4e89006" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186397647 :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(0)=0.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(0)=0,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186365897 :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+1}{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(0,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(1)=0.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(1)=0,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-c5f06778-8fd3-5821-8bf0-21b9cd17fe6a" :question/id #uuid "c5f06778-8fd3-5821-8bf0-21b9cd17fe6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-343296" :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=0.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(2)=0,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=-4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=4W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =4W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =4\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =4x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{e}^{4x} \\\\ W & =C{e}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =C{e}^{4\\cdot 2} \\\\ 0 & =C{e}^{8} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x\\in R.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5a400ebe-05b9-5fea-bc00-c6c0440a2abc" "answer-field-7dfca232-36a7-5cb3-b430-c23b013e76fb" "answer-field-966a0e85-1502-5c94-9acf-996294f09b8b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5a400ebe-05b9-5fea-bc00-c6c0440a2abc" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a400ebe-05b9-5fea-bc00-c6c0440a2abc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-687d92b4-4caa-5fc9-8d37-63128a791d4a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-efe3a6a0-86d9-5479-a4e0-cddc9da1b298" "answer-6045d0f1-c5e0-5e88-bf41-e0009edfbbd3" "answer-687d92b4-4caa-5fc9-8d37-63128a791d4a" "answer-a5c43a76-f128-5b7f-b097-5c194030e9ac"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-efe3a6a0-86d9-5479-a4e0-cddc9da1b298" :answer/id #uuid "efe3a6a0-86d9-5479-a4e0-cddc9da1b298" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6045d0f1-c5e0-5e88-bf41-e0009edfbbd3" :answer/id #uuid "6045d0f1-c5e0-5e88-bf41-e0009edfbbd3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4Q(x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-687d92b4-4caa-5fc9-8d37-63128a791d4a" :answer/id #uuid "687d92b4-4caa-5fc9-8d37-63128a791d4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5c43a76-f128-5b7f-b097-5c194030e9ac" :answer/id #uuid "a5c43a76-f128-5b7f-b097-5c194030e9ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4Q(x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7dfca232-36a7-5cb3-b430-c23b013e76fb" :answer-field/id #uuid "7dfca232-36a7-5cb3-b430-c23b013e76fb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-595167da-3acd-5df3-b394-03c41a45bf79" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ad2a0096-910d-5190-b55c-8f4b28f4a232" "answer-24395af1-9df4-5644-be8d-db6adebee26d" "answer-595167da-3acd-5df3-b394-03c41a45bf79" "answer-3c757474-ae67-5a54-adba-2dc1da22dd83"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad2a0096-910d-5190-b55c-8f4b28f4a232" :answer/id #uuid "ad2a0096-910d-5190-b55c-8f4b28f4a232" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Cx" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24395af1-9df4-5644-be8d-db6adebee26d" :answer/id #uuid "24395af1-9df4-5644-be8d-db6adebee26d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C{e}^{-4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-595167da-3acd-5df3-b394-03c41a45bf79" :answer/id #uuid "595167da-3acd-5df3-b394-03c41a45bf79" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C{e}^{4x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c757474-ae67-5a54-adba-2dc1da22dd83" :answer/id #uuid "3c757474-ae67-5a54-adba-2dc1da22dd83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "C{x}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-966a0e85-1502-5c94-9acf-996294f09b8b" :answer-field/id #uuid "966a0e85-1502-5c94-9acf-996294f09b8b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-6186a245-0035-5550-a011-2d8886fe37b9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6186a245-0035-5550-a011-2d8886fe37b9" "answer-7a9676d0-911f-5862-8941-5a14f3882472" "answer-41652d46-4e1b-554c-b2f6-2fa3e1ff95c2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6186a245-0035-5550-a011-2d8886fe37b9" :answer/id #uuid "6186a245-0035-5550-a011-2d8886fe37b9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a9676d0-911f-5862-8941-5a14f3882472" :answer/id #uuid "7a9676d0-911f-5862-8941-5a14f3882472" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x\\in R$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41652d46-4e1b-554c-b2f6-2fa3e1ff95c2" :answer/id #uuid "41652d46-4e1b-554c-b2f6-2fa3e1ff95c2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x\\in R$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186217801 :question/problem "$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\\,x>0\n$$\n\nLet ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ be solutions to the second-order homogeneous differential equation above, where $Q(x)$ is continuous on $(0,\\infty ).$ The Wronskian satisfies $W(2)=0.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies the following first-order differential equation:\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)={{field-1}}W(x)\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we get\n\n$$\nW={{field-2}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow, using the fact that $W(2)=0,$ we obtain \n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Abel's identity says that if ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their Wronskian $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of ${y}^{′}$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into Abel's identity gives\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{1}{x}W(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first-order differential equation for $W:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′} & =-\\frac{1}{x}W \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =-\\frac{1}{x}\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}W}{W} & =\\int -\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|W| & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+K \\\\ |W| & ={e}^{K}{x}^{-1} \\\\ W & =\\frac{C}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nNext, we use the given value $W(2)=0$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(2) & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{C}{2} \\\\ C & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $W(x)=0\\text{ for all }x>0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f5a05a66-bb84-597b-8204-53d4da9cb137" "answer-field-1458f4ab-9e6b-58ec-a4d4-41759c631919"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186216668 :answer-field/correct 17592186216671 :answer-field/choices ["answer-dc69df00-8902-553c-9a7e-a1f2b9bc5e67" "answer-1e804472-aa9d-546b-8e8f-780a182772a0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc69df00-8902-553c-9a7e-a1f2b9bc5e67" :answer/id #uuid "dc69df00-8902-553c-9a7e-a1f2b9bc5e67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{q(x)}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e804472-aa9d-546b-8e8f-780a182772a0" :answer/id #uuid "1e804472-aa9d-546b-8e8f-780a182772a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{x}{q(x)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f5a05a66-bb84-597b-8204-53d4da9cb137" :answer-field/id #uuid "f5a05a66-bb84-597b-8204-53d4da9cb137" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-9a9e47f8-5ce8-5599-9a19-800144afed11" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dcc27853-144c-5d1c-822c-2cb725c6bfdc" "answer-9a9e47f8-5ce8-5599-9a19-800144afed11" "answer-e249b4b4-00d4-5097-8015-f90a8f3174d3" "answer-5d9e74e1-73cc-5d9b-92c4-713de893186a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcc27853-144c-5d1c-822c-2cb725c6bfdc" :answer/id #uuid "dcc27853-144c-5d1c-822c-2cb725c6bfdc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{C}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a9e47f8-5ce8-5599-9a19-800144afed11" :answer/id #uuid "9a9e47f8-5ce8-5599-9a19-800144afed11" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{C}{x}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e249b4b4-00d4-5097-8015-f90a8f3174d3" :answer/id #uuid "e249b4b4-00d4-5097-8015-f90a8f3174d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{C}{{e}^{x}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d9e74e1-73cc-5d9b-92c4-713de893186a" :answer/id #uuid "5d9e74e1-73cc-5d9b-92c4-713de893186a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1458f4ab-9e6b-58ec-a4d4-41759c631919" :answer-field/id #uuid "1458f4ab-9e6b-58ec-a4d4-41759c631919" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-96cbb24e-680a-5a3a-9133-e2ae34a77ad1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-96cbb24e-680a-5a3a-9133-e2ae34a77ad1" "answer-cc020a0a-3e91-54e9-b3ff-3a8b6b0c9421" "answer-fb1a3557-e8a9-5474-820f-a54d260979a6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96cbb24e-680a-5a3a-9133-e2ae34a77ad1" :answer/id #uuid "96cbb24e-680a-5a3a-9133-e2ae34a77ad1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for all $x>0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc020a0a-3e91-54e9-b3ff-3a8b6b0c9421" :answer/id #uuid "cc020a0a-3e91-54e9-b3ff-3a8b6b0c9421" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$W(x)=0$ for some but not all $x>0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb1a3557-e8a9-5474-820f-a54d260979a6" :answer/id #uuid "fb1a3557-e8a9-5474-820f-a54d260979a6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$W(x)\\ne 0$ for all $x>0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186218358 :tutorial/content "The Wronskian is a quick way to check whether two solutions of a homogeneous ODE are linearly independent.\n\nIn this lesson, we learn an identity, which tells us how the Wronskian changes with $x$ for an equation of the form \n\n$$\ny''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0.\n$$\n\nSuppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the second-order linear homogeneous ODE above on some interval $I.$ Let $W(x)$ be their Wronskian:\n![](images/bf/bf982a342681e4142c476f52c77cb95836bc024d75a0c62961fc5f0d6d71320b.svg)\n\nThen, **Abel's identity** states that the Wronskian satisfies the first-order linear differential equation\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-P(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nSolving this equation via separation of variables gives the general form for $W(x){:}$\n\n$$\nW(x) = C e^{-\\int P(x) \\, \\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nThis allows us to determine the behavior of the Wronskian without knowing the solutions $y_1$ and $y_2.$\n\nFor example, consider the second-order homogeneous differential equation \n\n$$\ny''+3xy'+e^x y=0\n$$\n\nwith solutions $y_1$ and $y_2,$ and the corresponding Wronskian $W(x).$ Let's determine the differential equation that $W(x)$ must satisfy.\n\nIn our equation, the coefficient of $y'$ is \n\n$$\nP(x)=3x.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Abel's identity gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & =-3xW(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** If the equation is not already in standard form, we should rewrite it before applying Abel’s identity." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186218359 :tutorial/content "*Abel's identity* says that if $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of\n\n$$\ny''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0,\n$$\n\nthen their *Wronskian* $W(x)$ satisfies\n\n$$\nW'(x)=-p(x)W(x).\n$$\n\nTo prove it, we start with the definition\n\n$$\nW(x)=y_1y_2'-y_2y_1'\n$$\n\nand differentiate both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}}+{y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{″}-\\cancel{{y}_{2}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{″} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{″}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{″}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of $y''+p(x)y'+q(x)y=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny_1''=-p(x)y_1'-q(x)y_1\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ny_2''=-p(x)y_2'-q(x)y_2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute these into the expression for $W'(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}^{′}(x) & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{″}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{″} \\\\  & ={y}_{1}(-p(x){y}_{2}^{′}-q(x){y}_{2})-{y}_{2}(-p(x){y}_{1}^{′}-q(x){y}_{1}) \\\\  & =-p(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-\\cancel{q(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}}+p(x){y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}+\\cancel{q(x){y}_{1}{y}_{2}} \\\\  & =-p(x)({y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}) \\\\  & =-p(x)W(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $W'(x)=-p(x)W(x)$, as required." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186217326 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186369929 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x\\}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot 15My\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{x}{x}^{2}y+{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{y={x}^{2}}^{y=x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15M{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})-(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{8})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15M{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{4}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{6}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{8}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =15M{[\\frac{3}{20}{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{14}{x}^{7}-\\frac{1}{36}{x}^{9}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =15M(\\frac{3}{20}-\\frac{1}{14}-\\frac{1}{36}) \\\\  & =15M\\cdot \\frac{16}{315} \\\\  & =\\frac{16M}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the origin is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{0} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16M}{21}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{21}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{16}}{\\sqrt{21}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{21}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{21}}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281684 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3x\\}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3x}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{M}{45}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{45}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3x}{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{45}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{[{x}^{4}y+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{45}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3{x}^{5}+9{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{45}{\\int }_{1}^{2}12{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{45}[2{x}^{6}]_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{45}\\cdot (128-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{14M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{14}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186369936 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{y}={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{y} & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}{x}^{2}\\cdot 5Mx\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{3}}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{3}y]}_{y=0}^{y={x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5M{[\\frac{1}{7}{x}^{7}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =5M\\cdot \\frac{1}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{5M}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $y$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{5M}{7}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186281685 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,{x}^{3}\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{1}{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{28M}{3}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{28M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{3}}^{1}x{y}^{5}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{28M}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{6}x{y}^{6}]}_{y={x}^{3}}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{1}x-{x}^{19}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{9}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{20}{x}^{20}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{9}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{20}) \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{7M}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{7M}{10}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186284396 :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nIn polar coordinates, the region occupied by the plate is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe moment of inertia about the origin, in polar coordinates, is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{0}={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (r,\\theta )$ is the mass density function.\n\nAlso, the mass density in terms of polar coordinates is $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$\nTherefore, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{0} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta ){r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{5}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }({\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r)({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}{|}_{1}^{2}({\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\frac{4M}{15\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{21}{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{\\theta }{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{4}]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{5\\pi }\\cdot \\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{14M}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{0} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{14M}{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{14}{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-245d9117-62b6-5cd4-a894-dc26b3c85ce0" :activity/id #uuid "245d9117-62b6-5cd4-a894-dc26b3c85ce0" :activity/title "Calculating the Radius of Gyration of a Plane Lamina" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050741 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-c3e8f762-08b5-5abb-ad57-1ccd0f374959" "step-9d167502-9ba9-5a99-b76c-4bbb7ffd2127" "step-35c79eda-f94c-58a9-8374-46b32306129c" "step-abbacb62-fb28-5335-8088-680b344ce423" "step-427ca060-da3d-52bf-afe9-f9fcdd76bea6"] :activity/first-step "step-c3e8f762-08b5-5abb-ad57-1ccd0f374959" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-c3e8f762-08b5-5abb-ad57-1ccd0f374959" :step/id #uuid "c3e8f762-08b5-5abb-ad57-1ccd0f374959" :step/content 17592186369929 :step/next "step-9d167502-9ba9-5a99-b76c-4bbb7ffd2127" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9d167502-9ba9-5a99-b76c-4bbb7ffd2127" :step/id #uuid "9d167502-9ba9-5a99-b76c-4bbb7ffd2127" :step/content 17592186281684 :step/next "step-35c79eda-f94c-58a9-8374-46b32306129c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-35c79eda-f94c-58a9-8374-46b32306129c" :step/id #uuid "35c79eda-f94c-58a9-8374-46b32306129c" :step/content 17592186369936 :step/next "step-abbacb62-fb28-5335-8088-680b344ce423" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-abbacb62-fb28-5335-8088-680b344ce423" :step/id #uuid "abbacb62-fb28-5335-8088-680b344ce423" :step/content 17592186281685 :step/next "step-427ca060-da3d-52bf-afe9-f9fcdd76bea6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-427ca060-da3d-52bf-afe9-f9fcdd76bea6" :step/id #uuid "427ca060-da3d-52bf-afe9-f9fcdd76bea6" :step/content 17592186284396 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166752 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8b164922-7a86-5e35-904e-baea511723b4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8b164922-7a86-5e35-904e-baea511723b4" :question/id #uuid "8b164922-7a86-5e35-904e-baea511723b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-95012" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 7\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 8\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & 6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2} \\\\ 9{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} & 7{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -7 & 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ 9{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-7 \\\\ 7{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 9 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 9 & 1 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 8 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+(-\\frac{7}{6}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{13}{2} & -13 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{13}{6} & \\frac{13}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+5{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=2 \\\\ -\\frac{13}{2}{x}_{2}=-13 \\\\ \\frac{13}{6}{x}_{2}=\\frac{13}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second, third and fourth equations, we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+5\\cdot 2 & =4 \\\\ 6{x}_{1} & =-6 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1+2=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8fdc3716-f94a-5460-8503-fc942472a7e6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8fdc3716-f94a-5460-8503-fc942472a7e6" :answer-field/id #uuid "8fdc3716-f94a-5460-8503-fc942472a7e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d1293524-2d61-50b9-9952-84c3ff0dc264" :answer-field/choices ["answer-44cab1fc-6315-55b0-b3f7-05e725aa34f3" "answer-c58ad0a2-710d-5e98-bc14-5b4a2dc8be59" "answer-68265195-74f6-5911-84af-be7e3b4820a8" "answer-d1293524-2d61-50b9-9952-84c3ff0dc264" "answer-04ec09a2-fbda-5a52-bb47-467878875739"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44cab1fc-6315-55b0-b3f7-05e725aa34f3" :answer/id #uuid "44cab1fc-6315-55b0-b3f7-05e725aa34f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c58ad0a2-710d-5e98-bc14-5b4a2dc8be59" :answer/id #uuid "c58ad0a2-710d-5e98-bc14-5b4a2dc8be59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68265195-74f6-5911-84af-be7e3b4820a8" :answer/id #uuid "68265195-74f6-5911-84af-be7e3b4820a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1293524-2d61-50b9-9952-84c3ff0dc264" :answer/id #uuid "d1293524-2d61-50b9-9952-84c3ff0dc264" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04ec09a2-fbda-5a52-bb47-467878875739" :answer/id #uuid "04ec09a2-fbda-5a52-bb47-467878875739" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186360539 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} & -3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2} & 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ 9{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -6{x}_{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} & =1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =1-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{2}{3}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186366587 :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=1-3{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=-3t+{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=2-3t-5{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(1-3{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(-3t+{t}^{2}) & =2-3t-5{t}^{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}t+(-3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =2-3t-5{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=2 \\\\ -3{x}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations, we get ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2+1=3.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186360542 :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2{t}^{2}+t-2,\\,{p}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}+3t-6,\\,{p}_{3}(t)=-2{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2},\\,\\,q(t)=-2{t}^{3}+4{t}^{2}+3t-6.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t),{p}_{3}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t)=q(t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2{t}^{2}+t-2)+{x}_{2}({t}^{2}+3t-6)+{x}_{3}(-2{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}) & =-2{t}^{3}+4{t}^{2}+3t-6 \\\\ -2{x}_{3}{t}^{3}+(2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}){t}^{2}+({x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})t+(-2{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}) & =-2{t}^{3}+4{t}^{2}+3t-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=4 \\\\ {x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{3} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{4}:={R}_{4}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=3 \\\\ -5{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=-2 \\\\ -2{x}_{3}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=1.$ Substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-5{x}_{2}+3\\cdot 1=-2\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, substituting the obtained values into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+3\\cdot 1=3\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ and therefore ${x}_{2}=1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186243954 :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2+{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=1-t+2{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=6+2t.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2+{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(1-t+2{t}^{2}) & =6+2t \\\\ (2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})-{x}_{2}t+({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =6+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{3}{2} & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations we get ${x}_{2}=-2.$  Substituting this into the first equation yields\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+(-2)=6\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=4+(-2)=2.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-7a5a6986-cd36-531a-9f9c-dcfb6003f956" :activity/id #uuid "7a5a6986-cd36-531a-9f9c-dcfb6003f956" :activity/title "The Coordinates of a Vector Relative to a Basis in Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048333 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-eefaff6d-e472-53e9-a5c9-55ede9a20c93" "step-a74c6d5d-0020-5707-b38c-10b2bc08bb70" "step-336b9f6a-688f-5c80-8b64-72381caefc8e" "step-fe154e6d-10aa-5895-a8de-82e02aff4a99" "step-bb742e8c-2853-5793-a821-ef78c316058a"] :activity/first-step "step-eefaff6d-e472-53e9-a5c9-55ede9a20c93" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-eefaff6d-e472-53e9-a5c9-55ede9a20c93" :step/id #uuid "eefaff6d-e472-53e9-a5c9-55ede9a20c93" :step/content "question-8b164922-7a86-5e35-904e-baea511723b4" :step/next "step-a74c6d5d-0020-5707-b38c-10b2bc08bb70" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a74c6d5d-0020-5707-b38c-10b2bc08bb70" :step/id #uuid "a74c6d5d-0020-5707-b38c-10b2bc08bb70" :step/content 17592186360539 :step/next "step-336b9f6a-688f-5c80-8b64-72381caefc8e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-336b9f6a-688f-5c80-8b64-72381caefc8e" :step/id #uuid "336b9f6a-688f-5c80-8b64-72381caefc8e" :step/content 17592186366587 :step/next "step-fe154e6d-10aa-5895-a8de-82e02aff4a99" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-fe154e6d-10aa-5895-a8de-82e02aff4a99" :step/id #uuid "fe154e6d-10aa-5895-a8de-82e02aff4a99" :step/content 17592186360542 :step/next "step-bb742e8c-2853-5793-a821-ef78c316058a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-bb742e8c-2853-5793-a821-ef78c316058a" :step/id #uuid "bb742e8c-2853-5793-a821-ef78c316058a" :step/content 17592186243954 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186123740 :answer-field/choices 17592186123744] [:db/retract 17592186123740 :answer-field/choices 17592186123745] [:db/retract 17592186123740 :answer-field/choices 17592186123741] [:db/retract 17592186123740 :answer-field/choices 17592186123742] [:db/retract 17592186123740 :answer-field/choices 17592186123743] {:db/id 17592186125190 :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $xy={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xy & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-7 \\\\ 0 & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-xy-7)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x-y)\\,i+(2y-x)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(2(2)-(-1))\\,i+(2(-1)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =5\\,i-4\\,j \\\\  & =⟨5,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨5,-4⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{5} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{-4} \\\\ -4(x-2) & =5(y+1) \\\\ -4x-5y+8-5 & =0 \\\\ -4x-5y+3 & =0 \\\\ 4x+5y-3 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186123740 :answer-field/correct "answer-9a929f5c-99d1-523a-aabc-fdf5eb4c7f18" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6cc0248f-f177-570e-9398-26b743d6c5ad" "answer-bc272b8f-4ab5-5f01-8506-f12b4f57de0b" "answer-132dcf6f-8423-544a-b9b0-4fe0220a1869" "answer-034d9a67-8763-5c70-b5b9-7febdebfdaf9" "answer-9a929f5c-99d1-523a-aabc-fdf5eb4c7f18"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6cc0248f-f177-570e-9398-26b743d6c5ad" :answer/id #uuid "6cc0248f-f177-570e-9398-26b743d6c5ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2x-2y+3=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc272b8f-4ab5-5f01-8506-f12b4f57de0b" :answer/id #uuid "bc272b8f-4ab5-5f01-8506-f12b4f57de0b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5x-4y+1=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-132dcf6f-8423-544a-b9b0-4fe0220a1869" :answer/id #uuid "132dcf6f-8423-544a-b9b0-4fe0220a1869" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-x-3y+5=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-034d9a67-8763-5c70-b5b9-7febdebfdaf9" :answer/id #uuid "034d9a67-8763-5c70-b5b9-7febdebfdaf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3x-5y+1=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a929f5c-99d1-523a-aabc-fdf5eb4c7f18" :answer/id #uuid "9a929f5c-99d1-523a-aabc-fdf5eb4c7f18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4x+5y-3=0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ca2288f3-367d-50c0-9754-21f3665fdec7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ca2288f3-367d-50c0-9754-21f3665fdec7" :question/id #uuid "ca2288f3-367d-50c0-9754-21f3665fdec7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71406" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-xyz$ at the point $(1,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-xyz \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2).$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y+z)+xyz)k \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+yz)\\text{ }i+(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+xz)\\text{ }j+(\\frac{1}{x+y+z}+xy)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,0,1) & =(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(0)(1))\\,i+(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(1)(1))\\text{ }j+(\\frac{1}{1+0+1}+(1)(0))\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+\\frac{3}{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,0,1)$ and passes through the point $(1,0,1)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,∇f(1,0,1)=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-631be222-c76c-5ee5-9b25-874887015acd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-631be222-c76c-5ee5-9b25-874887015acd" :answer-field/id #uuid "631be222-c76c-5ee5-9b25-874887015acd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fbc71725-cf86-521b-8c43-ec05f48b2500" :answer-field/choices ["answer-399e3797-afa8-52bc-9d91-1f755a50f199" "answer-d59ec8f6-4eba-53b8-a3b8-5a5f6ce7e72d" "answer-fbc71725-cf86-521b-8c43-ec05f48b2500" "answer-367ab4f2-93bc-5938-9462-dc0e018dd590" "answer-db86fc5b-7216-54fe-8379-494c5e62c9fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-399e3797-afa8-52bc-9d91-1f755a50f199" :answer/id #uuid "399e3797-afa8-52bc-9d91-1f755a50f199" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨1,1,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d59ec8f6-4eba-53b8-a3b8-5a5f6ce7e72d" :answer/id #uuid "d59ec8f6-4eba-53b8-a3b8-5a5f6ce7e72d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbc71725-cf86-521b-8c43-ec05f48b2500" :answer/id #uuid "fbc71725-cf86-521b-8c43-ec05f48b2500" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=⟨1,0,1⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-367ab4f2-93bc-5938-9462-dc0e018dd590" :answer/id #uuid "367ab4f2-93bc-5938-9462-dc0e018dd590" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩+t\\,⟨1,0,1⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db86fc5b-7216-54fe-8379-494c5e62c9fd" :answer/id #uuid "db86fc5b-7216-54fe-8379-494c5e62c9fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "r=⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩+t\\,⟨\\frac{1}{2},0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-5705edea-b336-51ef-88f9-19f848c1f228" :activity/id #uuid "5705edea-b336-51ef-88f9-19f848c1f228" :activity/title "The Gradient as a Normal Vector" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050027 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-053dd984-66f7-5e60-a777-6ca3631e9fa4" "step-14aa383b-ac4e-5a2e-b93c-5310c25c9ba2" "step-bf1f0a54-a2e4-5d5b-9366-b7874e2b2325" "step-17889977-02c9-5363-abaf-c90dc6ba9c00" "step-648fbdd8-b44b-5888-8db1-2712803cf64d"] :activity/first-step "step-053dd984-66f7-5e60-a777-6ca3631e9fa4" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-053dd984-66f7-5e60-a777-6ca3631e9fa4" :step/id #uuid "053dd984-66f7-5e60-a777-6ca3631e9fa4" :step/content 17592186125190 :step/next "step-14aa383b-ac4e-5a2e-b93c-5310c25c9ba2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-14aa383b-ac4e-5a2e-b93c-5310c25c9ba2" :step/id #uuid "14aa383b-ac4e-5a2e-b93c-5310c25c9ba2" :step/content 17592186398954 :step/next "step-bf1f0a54-a2e4-5d5b-9366-b7874e2b2325" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-bf1f0a54-a2e4-5d5b-9366-b7874e2b2325" :step/id #uuid "bf1f0a54-a2e4-5d5b-9366-b7874e2b2325" :step/content "question-ca2288f3-367d-50c0-9754-21f3665fdec7" :step/next "step-17889977-02c9-5363-abaf-c90dc6ba9c00" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-17889977-02c9-5363-abaf-c90dc6ba9c00" :step/id #uuid "17889977-02c9-5363-abaf-c90dc6ba9c00" :step/content 17592186265960 :step/next "step-648fbdd8-b44b-5888-8db1-2712803cf64d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-648fbdd8-b44b-5888-8db1-2712803cf64d" :step/id #uuid "648fbdd8-b44b-5888-8db1-2712803cf64d" :step/content 17592186266050 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147421 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-519a949e-8b2c-53c0-bd01-456c0a3f29d3" "question-2f3a7c31-c6fb-510a-b749-2c21cfbaae09"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147176 :question/problem "The number of hedgehogs $N(t)$ in a particular nature reserve after $t$ years is modeled by the function\n\n$$\nN(t) = 30 \\cdot 2^t.\n$$\n\nHow many years will it take for the population of hedgehogs to increase to $7\\,680?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the time it takes for the number of hedgehogs to reach $7\\,680,$ we need to solve the equation $N(t)=7\\,680$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =7\\,680 \\\\ 30\\cdot {2}^{t} & =7\\,680 \\\\ {2}^{t} & =\\frac{7\\,680}{30} \\\\ {2}^{t} & =256 \\\\ {2}^{t} & ={2}^{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it will take $t=8$ years for the population of hedgehogs to increase to $7\\,680.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147422 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-74b32b7f-8020-5b1d-8650-2271e11205ea" "question-94ed1bcc-bdbb-5a68-affe-a9e769dd8927"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147177 :question/problem "Bob invests $\\[math]250$ into some high-growth stocks, and the value of his investment doubles every year. How long will it take for Bob's investment to increase to $\\[/math]16\\,000?$" :question/worked-solution "We know that Bob's investment doubles every year. So the value of Bob's investment, which we'll call $y(t),$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $2.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny(t) = ab^t.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=250$ is the initial investment (in dollars).\n\n- $b=2$ is the growth factor.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in years, since the investment was first made.\n\nTherefore, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t) = 250\\cdot 2^t.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the time taken for the investment to reach $\\[math]16\\,000,$  we need to solve the equation $y(t) = 16\\,000$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =16\\,000 \\\\ 250\\cdot {2}^{t} & =16\\,000 \\\\ {2}^{t} & =\\frac{16\\,000}{250} \\\\ {2}^{t} & =64 \\\\ {2}^{t} & ={2}^{6} \\\\ t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it will take $6$ years for Bob's investment to grow to $\\[/math]16\\,000.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147423 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-65185e0f-08b4-53fe-970a-61a3c4a92c37" "question-25c45b73-9502-506c-8f02-7a944a17847b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147178 :question/problem "Mary is conducting a scientific experiment with bacteria. She notices that the number of bacteria in a Petri dish grows by $15\\%$ each day. If there were initially $100$ bacteria in the dish, what equation does Mary need to solve to predict how long it will take for the number of bacteria to increase to $535?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of bacteria $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $15\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny = a(1+r)^t.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=100$ is the initial number of bacteria.\n\n- $r = 0.15$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in days, since the experiment is started.\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t) = 100\\cdot(1+0.15)^t = 100\\cdot (1.15)^t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the time it will take for the number of bacteria to reach $535,$ we would need to solve the equation $y(t)= 535,$ which gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100\\cdot (1.15)^{t} & =535.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147424 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0e669fba-a4ac-508f-acb9-2cbafeb51763" "question-8cbabf22-40c3-5c95-8c42-a48a71281e71"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147179 :question/problem "Some scientists are monitoring the growth of the wolf population in a particular nature reserve. The population of wolves $W(t)$ after $t$ decades of observations is given by\n\n$$\nW(t)  = W_{0} \\cdot 4^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $W_{0}$ represents the wolves' population at the beginning of the study. If there were $3\\,200$ wolves after $3$ decades, how many wolves were there at the beginning of the study?" :question/worked-solution "There are $3\\,200$  wolves  $3$ decades after the scientists started studying the wolf population, so $W(3)  = 3\\,200.$\n\nTo find the initial number of wolves $W_0,$ we substitute $t=3$ and $W(3)  = 3\\,200$ into the given equation and solve for $W_0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & ={W}_{0}\\cdot {4}^{3} \\\\ 3\\,200 & ={W}_{0}\\cdot 64 \\\\ {W}_{0} & =\\frac{3\\,200}{64} \\\\ {W}_{0} & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there were $50$ wolves at the beginning of the study." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-519a949e-8b2c-53c0-bd01-456c0a3f29d3" :question/id #uuid "519a949e-8b2c-53c0-bd01-456c0a3f29d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-61756" :question/problem "The number $N(t)$ of bees in a hive after $t$ months can be modeled by the function\n\n$$\nN(t)=5\\,000\\cdot {2}^{t}.\n$$\n\nHow many months will it take for the population of bees to increase to $40\\,000?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the time it takes for the number of bees to reach $40\\,000,$ we need to solve the equation $N(t)=40\\,000$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =40\\,000 \\\\ 5\\,000\\cdot {2}^{t} & =40\\,000 \\\\ {2}^{t} & =\\frac{40\\,000}{5\\,000} \\\\ {2}^{t} & =\\frac{40}{5} \\\\ {2}^{t} & =8 \\\\ {2}^{t} & ={2}^{3} \\\\ t & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of bees increases to $40\\,000$ after $3$ months." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0373459d-6a90-5e83-a06b-57aa4a31bdbb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0373459d-6a90-5e83-a06b-57aa4a31bdbb" :answer-field/id #uuid "0373459d-6a90-5e83-a06b-57aa4a31bdbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ef8a0689-c144-5e95-8b08-03494f30d0d9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6b71812c-5e4b-5153-9260-0e1084106734" "answer-77717bbb-4108-5347-b237-95ef0dfb283c" "answer-ef8a0689-c144-5e95-8b08-03494f30d0d9" "answer-3d474c02-8953-5638-9006-799781937e8a" "answer-c63d0890-0e3b-5478-b9f5-110e856e0845"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b71812c-5e4b-5153-9260-0e1084106734" :answer/id #uuid "6b71812c-5e4b-5153-9260-0e1084106734" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77717bbb-4108-5347-b237-95ef0dfb283c" :answer/id #uuid "77717bbb-4108-5347-b237-95ef0dfb283c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef8a0689-c144-5e95-8b08-03494f30d0d9" :answer/id #uuid "ef8a0689-c144-5e95-8b08-03494f30d0d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d474c02-8953-5638-9006-799781937e8a" :answer/id #uuid "3d474c02-8953-5638-9006-799781937e8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c63d0890-0e3b-5478-b9f5-110e856e0845" :answer/id #uuid "c63d0890-0e3b-5478-b9f5-110e856e0845" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2f3a7c31-c6fb-510a-b749-2c21cfbaae09" :question/id #uuid "2f3a7c31-c6fb-510a-b749-2c21cfbaae09" :question/math-academy-id "q-112257" :question/problem "A social media influencer had $100$ followers the day she created her account. Since then, the number $F(t)$ of followers after $t$ weeks can be modeled by the function\n\n$$\nF(t)=100\\cdot {3}^{t}.\n$$\n\nHow many weeks will it take for the followers to increase to $72\\,900?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the time it takes for the number of followers to increase to $72\\,900,$ we need to solve the equation $F(t)=72\\,900$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =72\\,900 \\\\ 100\\cdot {3}^{t} & =72\\,900 \\\\ {3}^{t} & =\\frac{72\\,900}{100} \\\\ {3}^{t} & =729 \\\\ {3}^{t} & ={3}^{6} \\\\ t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number of followers increases to $72\\,900$ after $6$ weeks." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-713da8a1-7660-5675-94ac-ca37f7a2d59d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-713da8a1-7660-5675-94ac-ca37f7a2d59d" :answer-field/id #uuid "713da8a1-7660-5675-94ac-ca37f7a2d59d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-effec5c8-d58c-5634-8d36-a27334f6e96b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8569f2c3-47f2-55dc-af22-e693e573aabd" "answer-be6d85cd-dc8f-54f0-bb5e-4be03a2c345a" "answer-f8f74bde-3c91-5669-b932-9a4c8c5d4ed6" "answer-effec5c8-d58c-5634-8d36-a27334f6e96b" "answer-d3bdd980-4b02-5c9d-87c0-3ae5bf423ddd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8569f2c3-47f2-55dc-af22-e693e573aabd" :answer/id #uuid "8569f2c3-47f2-55dc-af22-e693e573aabd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be6d85cd-dc8f-54f0-bb5e-4be03a2c345a" :answer/id #uuid "be6d85cd-dc8f-54f0-bb5e-4be03a2c345a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8f74bde-3c91-5669-b932-9a4c8c5d4ed6" :answer/id #uuid "f8f74bde-3c91-5669-b932-9a4c8c5d4ed6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-effec5c8-d58c-5634-8d36-a27334f6e96b" :answer/id #uuid "effec5c8-d58c-5634-8d36-a27334f6e96b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3bdd980-4b02-5c9d-87c0-3ae5bf423ddd" :answer/id #uuid "d3bdd980-4b02-5c9d-87c0-3ae5bf423ddd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-74b32b7f-8020-5b1d-8650-2271e11205ea" :question/id #uuid "74b32b7f-8020-5b1d-8650-2271e11205ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-45649" :question/problem "An economic study in Chile showed that its gross domestic product (GDP) increases fourfold each decade. If the GDP was $$0.5$ billion in $2010$, how many decades will it take for the GDP to increase to $$512$ billion?" :question/worked-solution "We know that the GDP increases fourfold each decade. So the GDP, which we'll call $y(t),$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $4.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=0.5$ billion dollars was the GDP in $2010.$\n\n- $b=4$ is the growth factor.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in decades, since $2010.$\n\nTherefore, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=0.5\\cdot {4}^{t}.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the time it takes for the GDP to increase to $$512$ billion, we need to solve the equation $y(t)=512$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =512 \\\\ 0.5\\cdot {4}^{t} & =512 \\\\ {4}^{t} & =\\frac{512}{0.5} \\\\ {4}^{t} & =1\\,024 \\\\ {4}^{t} & ={4}^{5} \\\\ t & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, it will take $5$ decades for the GDP to increase to $$512$ billion." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-73e9c3a9-039d-5389-8d3a-67841500f704"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-73e9c3a9-039d-5389-8d3a-67841500f704" :answer-field/id #uuid "73e9c3a9-039d-5389-8d3a-67841500f704" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-00d61264-f40f-5d03-8c04-a35fe010f7f3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fdcaf9ba-8932-5825-8a62-1358baee383a" "answer-d79c959b-480e-5606-b96e-1a1b0caa28f4" "answer-d9095599-b7ed-56a3-a93d-492035719742" "answer-00d61264-f40f-5d03-8c04-a35fe010f7f3" "answer-e19e08c9-87f0-537a-96a1-72e7dd131284"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fdcaf9ba-8932-5825-8a62-1358baee383a" :answer/id #uuid "fdcaf9ba-8932-5825-8a62-1358baee383a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d79c959b-480e-5606-b96e-1a1b0caa28f4" :answer/id #uuid "d79c959b-480e-5606-b96e-1a1b0caa28f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9095599-b7ed-56a3-a93d-492035719742" :answer/id #uuid "d9095599-b7ed-56a3-a93d-492035719742" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00d61264-f40f-5d03-8c04-a35fe010f7f3" :answer/id #uuid "00d61264-f40f-5d03-8c04-a35fe010f7f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e19e08c9-87f0-537a-96a1-72e7dd131284" :answer/id #uuid "e19e08c9-87f0-537a-96a1-72e7dd131284" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-94ed1bcc-bdbb-5a68-affe-a9e769dd8927" :question/id #uuid "94ed1bcc-bdbb-5a68-affe-a9e769dd8927" :question/math-academy-id "q-237311" :question/problem "The population of ducks living in a nature reserve triples every year. If there were $8$ ducks initially, how many years will it take for the population to increase to $5\\,832?$\n\nThe population of ducks increases to $5\\,832$ after{{field-1}}years." :question/worked-solution "We know that the duck population triples every year. Thus, the duck population, which we will call $y(t),$ grows exponentially with a growth factor of $3.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth factor $b$) is\n\n$$\ny=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=8$ is the initial population of ducks.\n\n- $b=3$ is the growth factor.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in years, since the beginning of the observation.\n\nTherefore, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\ny(t)=8\\cdot {3}^{t}.\n$$\n\nNow, to find the time it takes for the population of ducks to increase to $5\\,832,$ we need to solve the equation $y(t)=5\\,832$ for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =5\\,832 \\\\ 8\\cdot {3}^{t} & =5\\,832 \\\\ {3}^{t} & =\\frac{5\\,832}{8} \\\\ {3}^{t} & =729 \\\\ {3}^{t} & ={3}^{6} \\\\ t & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population of ducks increases to $5\\,832$ after $6$ years." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1e06da05-5e62-506a-b486-be82db39219e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1e06da05-5e62-506a-b486-be82db39219e" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e06da05-5e62-506a-b486-be82db39219e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4b86eb9f-4d78-54f3-9bcc-45d5fe13a16e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4b86eb9f-4d78-54f3-9bcc-45d5fe13a16e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b86eb9f-4d78-54f3-9bcc-45d5fe13a16e" :answer/id #uuid "4b86eb9f-4d78-54f3-9bcc-45d5fe13a16e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-65185e0f-08b4-53fe-970a-61a3c4a92c37" :question/id #uuid "65185e0f-08b4-53fe-970a-61a3c4a92c37" :question/math-academy-id "q-237332" :question/problem "Paul is conducting a scientific experiment with amoebas. He notices that the number of amoebas grows by  $65%$ each day. If there were initially $140$ amoebas, complete the following equation that we should solve to determine how long it will take for the number of amoebas to increase to $800.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,(${{field-2}}$)^{t}=800$" :question/worked-solution "The amoeba population $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $65%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=140$ is the initial number of amoebas.\n\n- $r=0.65$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in days, since the beginning of the observation.\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =140\\cdot (1+0.65)^{t} \\\\  & =140\\cdot (1.65)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the time it will take for the amoeba population to increase to $800,$ we would need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =800 \\\\ 140\\cdot (1.65)^{t} & =800.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-48fde31c-ad75-5542-a64f-929ac3239408" "answer-field-beff01d7-6cb0-54e0-bc71-2ce1a1269b34"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-48fde31c-ad75-5542-a64f-929ac3239408" :answer-field/id #uuid "48fde31c-ad75-5542-a64f-929ac3239408" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-470a32ac-1f39-5cd0-9165-8909dac7971c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-470a32ac-1f39-5cd0-9165-8909dac7971c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-470a32ac-1f39-5cd0-9165-8909dac7971c" :answer/id #uuid "470a32ac-1f39-5cd0-9165-8909dac7971c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "140" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-beff01d7-6cb0-54e0-bc71-2ce1a1269b34" :answer-field/id #uuid "beff01d7-6cb0-54e0-bc71-2ce1a1269b34" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f316582c-e7bf-5ff9-8e9a-83d59a1c3910" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f316582c-e7bf-5ff9-8e9a-83d59a1c3910"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f316582c-e7bf-5ff9-8e9a-83d59a1c3910" :answer/id #uuid "f316582c-e7bf-5ff9-8e9a-83d59a1c3910" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.65" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-25c45b73-9502-506c-8f02-7a944a17847b" :question/id #uuid "25c45b73-9502-506c-8f02-7a944a17847b" :question/math-academy-id "q-112309" :question/problem "A fungi culture increases by $30%$ each week. If the culture started with $5$ fungi, what equation should we solve to determine how many weeks will it take for the fungi culture to increase to $100?$" :question/worked-solution "The fungi culture $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $30%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=5$ is the initial number of fungi.\n\n- $r=0.3$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in weeks, since the culture started.\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =5\\cdot (1+0.3)^{t} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (1.3)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the number of weeks it takes for the fungi culture to increase to $100,$ we would need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =100 \\\\ 5\\cdot (1.3)^{t} & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-29893dcd-fc6d-57fa-af02-82a279b31b2a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-29893dcd-fc6d-57fa-af02-82a279b31b2a" :answer-field/id #uuid "29893dcd-fc6d-57fa-af02-82a279b31b2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-84866a2b-bc2b-526b-8d03-f790bfc3078a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c73780c0-4767-5594-b17d-a5c24ba647cb" "answer-d4fe32b5-a478-58d5-959e-0ea2936100a5" "answer-bfc19a1f-7ee5-5af0-81fb-257d6f3a4cd3" "answer-84866a2b-bc2b-526b-8d03-f790bfc3078a" "answer-942ab900-0884-5729-97ce-f1a6fbeb9244"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c73780c0-4767-5594-b17d-a5c24ba647cb" :answer/id #uuid "c73780c0-4767-5594-b17d-a5c24ba647cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(5\\cdot 1.3)^{t}=100" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4fe32b5-a478-58d5-959e-0ea2936100a5" :answer/id #uuid "d4fe32b5-a478-58d5-959e-0ea2936100a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\cdot (0.3)^{t}=100" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfc19a1f-7ee5-5af0-81fb-257d6f3a4cd3" :answer/id #uuid "bfc19a1f-7ee5-5af0-81fb-257d6f3a4cd3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\cdot (1.03)^{t}=100" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84866a2b-bc2b-526b-8d03-f790bfc3078a" :answer/id #uuid "84866a2b-bc2b-526b-8d03-f790bfc3078a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\cdot (1.3)^{t}=100" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-942ab900-0884-5729-97ce-f1a6fbeb9244" :answer/id #uuid "942ab900-0884-5729-97ce-f1a6fbeb9244" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\cdot {3}^{t}=100" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0e669fba-a4ac-508f-acb9-2cbafeb51763" :question/id #uuid "0e669fba-a4ac-508f-acb9-2cbafeb51763" :question/math-academy-id "q-112315" :question/problem "In a certain country, the number of people $P(t)$ who know a second language, $t$ years after $2005,$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\cdot {3}^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ was the number of people who knew a second language in $2005.$ If in $2010$ the number of people who knew a second language was $4\\,860\\,000,$ how many people knew a second language in $2005?$" :question/worked-solution "The number of people who knew a second language in $2010$ was $4\\,860\\,000,$ this is $5$ years after $2005.$ Thus, $P(5)=4\\,860\\,000.$\n\nTo find ${P}_{0},$ the number of people who knew a second lenguage in $2005,$  we substitute $t=5$ and $P(5)=4\\,860\\,000$ into the given equation and solve for ${P}_{0},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5) & ={P}_{0}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ 4\\,860\\,000 & ={P}_{0}\\cdot {3}^{5} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{4\\,860\\,000}{243} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =20\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in $2005$ there were $20\\,000$ people who knew a second language in that country." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7aa37ed2-9413-56be-8870-8d9839afae71"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7aa37ed2-9413-56be-8870-8d9839afae71" :answer-field/id #uuid "7aa37ed2-9413-56be-8870-8d9839afae71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-123a1ad9-baf5-5e6e-bac2-3666b9f37fc0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d028f0b1-d088-54a7-9ec8-02a5463da751" "answer-123a1ad9-baf5-5e6e-bac2-3666b9f37fc0" "answer-34be7b39-3787-56c4-a8a1-cc4816e6c5e9" "answer-4b9470ef-ff15-5d7b-bfe8-1989c9744591" "answer-176f851e-2ff0-5aa9-94f6-f174585b3bc5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d028f0b1-d088-54a7-9ec8-02a5463da751" :answer/id #uuid "d028f0b1-d088-54a7-9ec8-02a5463da751" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "200\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-123a1ad9-baf5-5e6e-bac2-3666b9f37fc0" :answer/id #uuid "123a1ad9-baf5-5e6e-bac2-3666b9f37fc0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34be7b39-3787-56c4-a8a1-cc4816e6c5e9" :answer/id #uuid "34be7b39-3787-56c4-a8a1-cc4816e6c5e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b9470ef-ff15-5d7b-bfe8-1989c9744591" :answer/id #uuid "4b9470ef-ff15-5d7b-bfe8-1989c9744591" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-176f851e-2ff0-5aa9-94f6-f174585b3bc5" :answer/id #uuid "176f851e-2ff0-5aa9-94f6-f174585b3bc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,000" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8cbabf22-40c3-5c95-8c42-a48a71281e71" :question/id #uuid "8cbabf22-40c3-5c95-8c42-a48a71281e71" :question/math-academy-id "q-112316" :question/problem "The profits of a store $P(t),$ $t$ years after $2018,$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(t)={P}_{0}\\cdot (1.1)^{t},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{0}$ was the profit in $2018.$ If the profits in $2020$ were of $$48\\,400,$ what was the profit in $2018?$" :question/worked-solution "The store profits in $2020$ were of $$48\\,400,$ this is $2$ years after $2018.$ Thus, $P(2)=48\\,400.$\n\nTo find the profit ${P}_{0}$ in $2018,$  we substitute $t=2$ and $P(2)=48\\,400$ into the given equation and solve for ${P}_{0},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & ={P}_{0}\\cdot (1.1)^{2} \\\\ 48\\,400 & ={P}_{0}\\cdot (1.1)^{2} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =\\frac{48\\,400}{1.21} \\\\ {P}_{0} & =40\\,000\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profits in $2018$ were of $$40\\,000.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cb1457d4-2953-5025-b958-130faff94c81"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cb1457d4-2953-5025-b958-130faff94c81" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb1457d4-2953-5025-b958-130faff94c81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ea4814a9-6d0e-5452-8762-e165bb579db2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4054edd0-bb58-5d0d-8e3f-29eefdeffe97" "answer-1c6ae88e-4e51-540f-8e0d-0e267fff6e6f" "answer-a9c9d171-29f0-595c-94c3-48740f780d79" "answer-ea4814a9-6d0e-5452-8762-e165bb579db2" "answer-6c95ca80-b0c5-5fe3-901a-fbd6f43e7164"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4054edd0-bb58-5d0d-8e3f-29eefdeffe97" :answer/id #uuid "4054edd0-bb58-5d0d-8e3f-29eefdeffe97" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$10\\,000$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c6ae88e-4e51-540f-8e0d-0e267fff6e6f" :answer/id #uuid "1c6ae88e-4e51-540f-8e0d-0e267fff6e6f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$11\\,000$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9c9d171-29f0-595c-94c3-48740f780d79" :answer/id #uuid "a9c9d171-29f0-595c-94c3-48740f780d79" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$1\\,000$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea4814a9-6d0e-5452-8762-e165bb579db2" :answer/id #uuid "ea4814a9-6d0e-5452-8762-e165bb579db2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$40\\,000$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c95ca80-b0c5-5fe3-901a-fbd6f43e7164" :answer/id #uuid "6c95ca80-b0c5-5fe3-901a-fbd6f43e7164" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$4\\,000$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148795 :tutorial/content "We can use our knowledge of solving exponential equations to obtain information about a system that follows an exponential growth model.\n\nFor example, suppose that the revenue $R(t),$ in millions of dollars, of a rapidly growing startup company is given by the function\n\n$$\nR(t) = 2\\cdot 3^t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the number of years since the company was incorporated. How long will it take for the company's revenue to reach $\\[math]18$ million?\n\nTo find the solution to this problem, we need to solve the equation $R(t) = 18.$ This gives us the equation\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 3^t = 18.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we first divide both sides by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot {3}^{t} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot {3}^{t}}{2} & =\\frac{18}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}\\cdot {3}^{t}}{\\cancel{2}} & =9 \\\\ {3}^{t} & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we notice that $9$ is a power of $3,$ because $9=3^2.$ So we get\n\n$$\n3^{\\color{blue}t} = 3^{\\color{blue}2}.\n$$\n\nThe bases of both exponents are equal, so the powers must also be equal. Therefore, our solution is\n\n$$\nt = {\\color{blue}2}.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that it will take the company $\\color{blue}2$ years to reach a revenue of $\\[/math]18$ million." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148796 :tutorial/content "We often need to know the system's state at $t=0$ when using an exponential growth model.\n\nFor example, suppose that the number of users $N(t)$ of a new phone app can be modeled by the function\n\n$$\nN(t) = N_0(1.2)^t,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ represents the time, in weeks, since the app launched, and $N_0$ is a constant. If there are $5\\,000$ users $5$ weeks after launch, can we determine how many users the app had when it launched?\n\nHere, $N_0$ represents the initial number of users, and this is what we're trying to find.\n\nFrom the given information, we know that $N(5) = 5\\,000,$ So let's plug this into our function:\n\n$$\n5\\,000 = N_0(1.2)^5\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we can divide both sides by $(1.2)^5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^{5}} & =\\frac{{N}_{0}(1.2)^{5}}{(1.2)^{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^{5}} & =\\frac{{N}_{0}\\cancel{(1.2)^{5}}}{\\cancel{(1.2)^{5}}} \\\\ \\frac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^{5}} & ={N}_{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's flip the equation around to make it easier to work with. \n\n$$\nN_0 = \\dfrac{5\\,000}{(1.2)^5}\n$$\n\nWe can now work out $N_0$ using a calculator. We find that\n\n$$\nN_0\\approx 2\\,009.\n$$\n\nWe have rounded to the nearest integer since $N_0$ refers to a number of people.\n\nTherefore, when it launched, the app had $2\\,009$ users." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147664 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166927 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-54e98471-5688-5240-867b-3e9eae0b3046"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-54e98471-5688-5240-867b-3e9eae0b3046" :question/id #uuid "54e98471-5688-5240-867b-3e9eae0b3046" :question/math-academy-id "q-335588" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=4x(1-0.25x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-y+xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x(1-0.25x)-3xy=0 \\\\ -y+xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-y+xy & =0 \\\\ y(-1+x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=1.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x(1-0.25x)-3x(0) & =0 \\\\ 4x(0.25x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(4,0).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(1)(1-0.25(1))-3(1)y & =0 \\\\ 3-3y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(4,0),\\,(1,1).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a9119afb-d7f2-5573-9b5d-ea702cbc65cd" "answer-field-2827ddbf-479b-5003-a2de-01715d6851fe" "answer-field-866fb4cc-629f-52ec-a9a5-882783d95536"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a9119afb-d7f2-5573-9b5d-ea702cbc65cd" :answer-field/id #uuid "a9119afb-d7f2-5573-9b5d-ea702cbc65cd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-56c313eb-74f2-5951-b106-ca3f0b7a9669" :answer-field/choices ["answer-56c313eb-74f2-5951-b106-ca3f0b7a9669"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56c313eb-74f2-5951-b106-ca3f0b7a9669" :answer/id #uuid "56c313eb-74f2-5951-b106-ca3f0b7a9669" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2827ddbf-479b-5003-a2de-01715d6851fe" :answer-field/id #uuid "2827ddbf-479b-5003-a2de-01715d6851fe" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ac7f7168-2238-5278-920a-322ccf51d756" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ac7f7168-2238-5278-920a-322ccf51d756"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac7f7168-2238-5278-920a-322ccf51d756" :answer/id #uuid "ac7f7168-2238-5278-920a-322ccf51d756" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-866fb4cc-629f-52ec-a9a5-882783d95536" :answer-field/id #uuid "866fb4cc-629f-52ec-a9a5-882783d95536" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7038e05b-62e6-5b9d-89f6-5884cc66e3ab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7038e05b-62e6-5b9d-89f6-5884cc66e3ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7038e05b-62e6-5b9d-89f6-5884cc66e3ab" :answer/id #uuid "7038e05b-62e6-5b9d-89f6-5884cc66e3ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186370416 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=4x(1-0.25x)-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. The equilibria of the model are the following:\n\n$(0,0),\\,(${{field-1}}$,0),\\,(${{field-2}}$,${{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x(1-0.25x)-2xy=0 \\\\ -6y+3xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6y+3xy & =0 \\\\ y(-6+3x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x(1-0.25x)-2x(0) & =0 \\\\ 4x(0.25x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(4,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2)(1-0.25(2))-2(2)y & =0 \\\\ 4-4y & =0 \\\\ y & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2,1).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(4,0),\\,(2,1).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166928 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-af33fe07-4f86-5eae-9df8-9d1a0ae2b710"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-af33fe07-4f86-5eae-9df8-9d1a0ae2b710" :question/id #uuid "af33fe07-4f86-5eae-9df8-9d1a0ae2b710" :question/math-academy-id "q-335618" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-x)-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-2y+3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-x)-2xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2y+3xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-x)-2xy)=2-4x-2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-x)-2xy)=-2x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+3xy)=3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+3xy)=-2+3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}2-4x-2y & -2x \\\\ 3y & -2+3x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2-4(0)-2(0) & -2(0) \\\\ 3(0) & -2+3(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-feee918a-d250-5e87-b14c-0c111d8be8f2" "answer-field-00fdc5c7-d728-5093-9485-058b327df4f0" "answer-field-5c0a9ccb-e656-59b5-9c7d-a9c8e8844b5a" "answer-field-406f04cd-82c8-5e08-86d2-0fcb4bf15669" "answer-field-1856dbb2-897a-59dc-a013-64c7ea9ef3fe" "answer-field-6e38aff8-117f-5d18-a8ef-dfee327a427b" "answer-field-963b2689-75ac-584f-b914-bf153ce7d339"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-feee918a-d250-5e87-b14c-0c111d8be8f2" :answer-field/id #uuid "feee918a-d250-5e87-b14c-0c111d8be8f2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-c70a6098-ad96-5098-b4cd-e316a6f02a0e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8314a3d1-ca2d-5375-bba8-185f826d8883" "answer-f70d67a8-7c41-5b09-8d78-191af0485ec3" "answer-57093fa8-b5a6-51a4-91fe-a596706e217d" "answer-c70a6098-ad96-5098-b4cd-e316a6f02a0e" "answer-e8ef1fe8-f6c3-5596-a93b-23565d02f42b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8314a3d1-ca2d-5375-bba8