r-field/id #uuid "963b2689-75ac-584f-b914-bf153ce7d339" :answer-field/key "field-7" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-bfc60e22-f056-5e38-acee-a1ff3bd5b359" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e9fac3b2-a967-5d13-a1ea-4935f33399bb" "answer-bfc60e22-f056-5e38-acee-a1ff3bd5b359" "answer-8fd25b8b-25db-5c2b-b86f-30366fb4d881" "answer-0eec1027-c458-503e-ad55-303a4da9d908"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9fac3b2-a967-5d13-a1ea-4935f33399bb" :answer/id #uuid "e9fac3b2-a967-5d13-a1ea-4935f33399bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "center" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfc60e22-f056-5e38-acee-a1ff3bd5b359" :answer/id #uuid "bfc60e22-f056-5e38-acee-a1ff3bd5b359" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "saddle" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fd25b8b-25db-5c2b-b86f-30366fb4d881" :answer/id #uuid "8fd25b8b-25db-5c2b-b86f-30366fb4d881" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "spiral sink" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0eec1027-c458-503e-ad55-303a4da9d908" :answer/id #uuid "0eec1027-c458-503e-ad55-303a4da9d908" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "spiral source" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166929 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f33a0982-c501-5349-ad00-235127306e08"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f33a0982-c501-5349-ad00-235127306e08" :question/id #uuid "f33a0982-c501-5349-ad00-235127306e08" :question/math-academy-id "q-335670" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=5x(1-2x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-2y+3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0,\\,0]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=5x(1-2x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2y+3xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5x(1-2x)-xy)=5-20x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5x(1-2x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+3xy)=3y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+3xy)=-2+3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}5-20x-y & -x \\\\ 3y & -2+3x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}5-20(0)-0 & -0 \\\\ 3(0) & -2+3(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5\\text{ and }{\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ Since the eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is $\\text{an unstable}$ $\\text{saddle}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-da022dfa-88fa-58f1-8013-b75ae78ec5de" "answer-field-a9ab6cc7-1c6a-564f-9adc-c5736d5bdf19" "answer-field-0e5dedda-7f63-5622-b085-90aa8148b6a9" "answer-field-fbaea06f-9399-5f51-8fd6-1c23afa989f4" "answer-field-8acc6622-afda-55d1-9fdd-b74846689d1c" "answer-field-4bb23220-f