"7dbc97d0-d2e5-5a31-9865-e4bc3b4b7c2e" :activity/title "The Lotka-Volterra Model With Carrying Capacity" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049289 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-bdfcda17-c79f-53ce-af4f-1ad4c7ab8581" "step-9c6eefcd-5b2a-58f2-8fa4-3bbf0349123c" "step-51e6cd51-40b0-57dc-ae60-04efd51a77f5" "step-5932a145-7c28-5526-882d-17efb83e0fb8"] :activity/first-step "step-bdfcda17-c79f-53ce-af4f-1ad4c7ab8581" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-bdfcda17-c79f-53ce-af4f-1ad4c7ab8581" :step/id #uuid "bdfcda17-c79f-53ce-af4f-1ad4c7ab8581" :step/content "question-54e98471-5688-5240-867b-3e9eae0b3046" :step/next "step-9c6eefcd-5b2a-58f2-8fa4-3bbf0349123c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9c6eefcd-5b2a-58f2-8fa4-3bbf0349123c" :step/id #uuid "9c6eefcd-5b2a-58f2-8fa4-3bbf0349123c" :step/content 17592186370416 :step/next "step-51e6cd51-40b0-57dc-ae60-04efd51a77f5" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-51e6cd51-40b0-57dc-ae60-04efd51a77f5" :step/id #uuid "51e6cd51-40b0-57dc-ae60-04efd51a77f5" :step/content "question-af33fe07-4f86-5eae-9df8-9d1a0ae2b710" :step/next "step-5932a145-7c28-5526-882d-17efb83e0fb8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5932a145-7c28-5526-882d-17efb83e0fb8" :step/id #uuid "5932a145-7c28-5526-882d-17efb83e0fb8" :step/content "question-f33a0982-c501-5349-ad00-235127306e08" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229191 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-41d2dc29-ff79-5299-80eb-91a0d11f7713" "question-74d3e4f5-83dd-544e-8625-a8bd48421738"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-41d2dc29-ff79-5299-80eb-91a0d11f7713" :question/id #uuid "41d2dc29-ff79-5299-80eb-91a0d11f7713" :question/math-academy-id "q-334970" :question/problem "Given that $J(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${J}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${J}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute ${J}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nJ(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{J}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}] & =2(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =2(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t){\\int }_{0}^{1}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}3x\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2}{|}_{0}^{1}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(\\frac{3}{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(3-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-624d06da-e063-516f-b272-d7b29fea68c7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-624d06da-e063-516f-b272-d7b29fea68c7" :answer-field/id #uuid "624d06da-e063-516f-b272-d7b29fea68c7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-5ca15a3a-dee4-579f-a997-69fcee973c4e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5ca15a3a-dee4-579f-a997-69fcee973c4e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ca15a3a-dee4-579f-a997-69fcee973c4e" :answer/id #uuid "5ca15a3a-dee4-579f-a997-69fcee973c4e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3-2\\cos t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186320492 :answer-field/correct "answer-86c9e8ab-6efc-512b-8e57-f76adb3a22c9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-86c9e8ab-6efc-512b-8e57-f76adb3a22c9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86c9e8ab-6efc-512b-8e57-f76adb3a22c9" :answer/id #uuid "86c9e8ab-6efc-512b-8e57-f76adb3a22c9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20t^{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-74d3e4f5-83dd-544e-8625-a8bd48421738" :question/id #uuid "74d3e4f5-83dd-544e-8625-a8bd48421738" :question/math-academy-id "q-352889" :question/problem "Given that $K(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${K}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${K}^{′}(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(\\,$\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute ${K}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nK(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{K}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{3}] & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}-3(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t){\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral. We first expand the squared term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({x}^{2}+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1}+x{cos}^{2}⁡(t){|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3}))+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))+({cos}^{2}⁡(t)-(-{cos}^{2}⁡(t))) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}+2{cos}^{2}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{K}^{′}(t) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}+2{cos}^{2}⁡(t)) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)(1+3{cos}^{2}⁡(t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3d913041-76e7-5e6e-a8c0-9a8b344b6d06"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3d913041-76e7-5e6e-a8c0-9a8b344b6d06" :answer-field/id #uuid "3d913041-76e7-5e6e-a8c0-9a8b344b6d06" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b82e1968-237d-535e-9699-f7e822b282a0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b82e1968-237d-535e-9699-f7e822b282a0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b82e1968-237d-535e-9699-f7e822b282a0" :answer/id #uuid "b82e1968-237d-535e-9699-f7e822b282a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1+3\\cos^{2}(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-8b065634-803f-5739-9336-86488fa97262" :activity/id #uuid "8b065634-803f-5739-9336-86488fa97262" :activity/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048817 :activity/course 17592186046659 :activity/steps ["step-d4624cb3-279b-58e4-be04-71f50c9f1d95" "step-7fea924a-9132-5c8e-8405-bb171905179c" "step-fdb087f1-efc2-5793-8940-c3ea24f5ca43" "step-6b9aec71-5349-5890-880d-e9cca6269db7" "step-5f1e8c45-aac6-55f1-8386-0597586bbb27"] :activity/first-step "step-d4624cb3-279b-58e4-be04-71f50c9f1d95" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-d4624cb3-279b-58e4-be04-71f50c9f1d95" :step/id #uuid "d4624cb3-279b-58e4-be04-71f50c9f1d95" :step/content "question-41d2dc29-ff79-5299-80eb-91a0d11f7713" :step/next "step-7fea924a-9132-5c8e-8405-bb171905179c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7fea924a-9132-5c8e-8405-bb171905179c" :step/id #uuid "7fea924a-9132-5c8e-8405-bb171905179c" :step/content 17592186311557 :step/next "step-fdb087f1-efc2-5793-8940-c3ea24f5ca43" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-fdb087f1-efc2-5793-8940-c3ea24f5ca43" :step/id #uuid "fdb087f1-efc2-5793-8940-c3ea24f5ca43" :step/content 17592186311558 :step/next "step-6b9aec71-5349-5890-880d-e9cca6269db7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6b9aec71-5349-5890-880d-e9cca6269db7" :step/id #uuid "6b9aec71-5349-5890-880d-e9cca6269db7" :step/content 17592186320721 :step/next "step-5f1e8c45-aac6-55f1-8386-0597586bbb27" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5f1e8c45-aac6-55f1-8386-0597586bbb27" :step/id #uuid "5f1e8c45-aac6-55f1-8386-0597586bbb27" :step/content "question-74d3e4f5-83dd-544e-8625-a8bd48421738" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152142 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8bf9a5c9-633d-5f44-9ff1-68e67c6de9ca" "question-e991c075-8360-56e0-853e-991e6e8ee2ac" "question-6da6ef13-a309-5cf7-9df2-7ba14aab3c75"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151827 :question/problem "| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $6$ | $4$ |\n| $9$ | $6$ |\n| $15$ | $10$ |\n\nThe table above shows a proportional relationship. Assuming that $y=kx$ for each pair of values, what is the constant of proportionality?" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nTo find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(6,4),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table.\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{4÷2}{6÷2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $k=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152143 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-07ebc64d-3fda-57ce-9860-9ad032b9cd27"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151828 :question/problem "Martha is running low on cupcakes, so she decides to make some more. The amount of time $h$ (in hours) she spends baking and the number of cupcakes $c$ she makes are shown in the table below.\n\n| Hours $(h)$ | Cupcakes $(c)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $5$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ |\n| $4$ | $9$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. There is a proportional relationship between $c$ and $h.$\n\nII. There is **not** a proportional relationship between $c$ and $h.$\n\nIII. The constant of proportionality is $\\dfrac{9}{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's add a new column to our table and compute the ratio of cupcakes per hour for each row.\n\n| Hours $(h)$ | Cupcakes $(c)$ | Cupcakes / Hours |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $5$ | $\\dfrac 5 2$ |\n| $3$ | $7$ | $\\dfrac 7 3$ |\n| $4$ | $9$ | $\\dfrac 9 4$ |\n\nThe ratio of cupcakes per hour is not the same for each row. Therefore, there is *not* a proportional relationship between $c$ and $h.$ Consequently, there is no constant of proportionality.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152144 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-93ed9bdb-28a0-5b9b-a66a-358447792b6e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151829 :question/problem "Merlin is making sandwiches for a party. There is a proportional relationship between the time $x$ (in minutes) he spends preparing sandwiches and the number of sandwiches $y$ he makes.\n\n| Time $(x)$ | Sandwiches $(y)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $4$ |\n| $10$ | $8$ |\n| $15$ | $12$ |\n\nWhat is the equation for the relationship between $x$ and $y?$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nEvery proportional relationship can be written in the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(5,4),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table.\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality as follows:\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x} = \\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation relating $x$ and $y$ is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{4}{5} x.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152145 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7efe1e34-f652-5c85-ae54-64066cf92fb9" "question-338f58ff-827c-5814-b929-70f92d28e890" "question-111207e8-0baf-57b4-8619-90f5c5dcb6bf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151830 :question/problem "Jill likes to listen to music while she runs. The following table shows a proportional relationship between the distance $d$ (in kilometers) she runs and the number of songs $s$ she listens to.\n\n| Kilometers ($d$) | Songs ($s$) |\n| --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ |\n| $4$ | $6$ |\n| $6$ | $9$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The unit rate is  $\\dfrac32$ songs per kilometer.\n\nII. If Jill runs for $1$ kilometer, she'll listen to $1.5$ songs.\n\nIII. If Jill runs for $12$ kilometers, she'll listen to $15$ songs." :question/worked-solution "We're told that $d$ and $s$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nTo find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(d,s)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(2,3),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table. \n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality as follows:\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{s}{d} = \\dfrac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThe units of $k$ are \"songs per kilometer\" since we divided the number of songs $s$ by the distance $d.$ Therefore, the relationship between $d$ and $s$ is \n\n$$\ns=\\dfrac32 d.\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. We found above that $k =\\dfrac32$ songs per kilometer.\n\n- Statement II is true. We know that $k=\\dfrac32,$ which means that if Jill runs for $1$ kilometer, she'll listen to $\\dfrac32 = 1.5$ songs.\n\n- Statement III is false. If we substitute $d=12$ into the relationship $s=\\dfrac32 d,$ we get \n\n$$\ns=\\dfrac32\\cdot  12=18\n$$\n\nwhich means that she'll listen to $18$ songs if she runs for $12$ kilometers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152520 :question/problem "| $u$ | $v$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $6$ | $8$ |\n| $9$ | $12$ |\n| $12$ | $16$ |\n\nThe table above shows a proportional relationship. Assuming that $v=ku$ for each pair of values, what is the constant of proportionality? \n\nThe constant of proportionality is \n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $u$ and $v$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nTo find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(u,v)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(6,8),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table.\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $v$-coordinate to the $u$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{v}{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{8÷2}{6÷2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $k=\\frac{4}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186149614 :answer-field/correct "answer-8f5707b0-b50e-554f-9516-6acedd66f982" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8f5707b0-b50e-554f-9516-6acedd66f982"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f5707b0-b50e-554f-9516-6acedd66f982" :answer/id #uuid "8f5707b0-b50e-554f-9516-6acedd66f982" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8bf9a5c9-633d-5f44-9ff1-68e67c6de9ca" :question/id #uuid "8bf9a5c9-633d-5f44-9ff1-68e67c6de9ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-235781" :question/problem "| $u$ | $v$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $12$ | $15$ |\n| $16$ | $20$ |\n| $20$ | $25$ |\n\nThe table above shows a proportional relationship.  Assuming that $v=ku$ for each pair of values, what is the constant of proportionality? \n\nThe constant of proportionality is \n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $u$ and $v$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nTo find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(u,v)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(12,15),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table.\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $v$-coordinate to the $u$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{v}{u} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15÷3}{12÷3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $k=\\frac{5}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-28b7b681-5edb-5f7b-892b-92dc443c9e18"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-28b7b681-5edb-5f7b-892b-92dc443c9e18" :answer-field/id #uuid "28b7b681-5edb-5f7b-892b-92dc443c9e18" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c3495911-5cb0-5681-b52a-ce26a450aa47" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c3495911-5cb0-5681-b52a-ce26a450aa47"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3495911-5cb0-5681-b52a-ce26a450aa47" :answer/id #uuid "c3495911-5cb0-5681-b52a-ce26a450aa47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e991c075-8360-56e0-853e-991e6e8ee2ac" :question/id #uuid "e991c075-8360-56e0-853e-991e6e8ee2ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-68032" :question/problem "| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $8$ | $6$ |\n| $12$ | $9$ |\n| $16$ | $12$ |\n\nThe table above shows a proportional relationship. What is the constant of proportionality?" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nTo find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(8,6),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table.\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{6÷2}{8÷2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $k=\\frac{3}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-48308a8f-a15b-55d2-9c25-8275b1c10566"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-48308a8f-a15b-55d2-9c25-8275b1c10566" :answer-field/id #uuid "48308a8f-a15b-55d2-9c25-8275b1c10566" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-bfaa76f8-a44f-5b16-b825-b6478d1f6808" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d8bcbb05-5da2-5e97-b537-afb582cdbbc6" "answer-0f688d6b-922e-58ef-bcb3-12d4f73d4d03" "answer-dcca470d-2ab6-5064-a4d0-573594493d95" "answer-bfaa76f8-a44f-5b16-b825-b6478d1f6808" "answer-f009da6d-a329-5d22-a54b-3ee89ff22e7c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8bcbb05-5da2-5e97-b537-afb582cdbbc6" :answer/id #uuid "d8bcbb05-5da2-5e97-b537-afb582cdbbc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f688d6b-922e-58ef-bcb3-12d4f73d4d03" :answer/id #uuid "0f688d6b-922e-58ef-bcb3-12d4f73d4d03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcca470d-2ab6-5064-a4d0-573594493d95" :answer/id #uuid "dcca470d-2ab6-5064-a4d0-573594493d95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bfaa76f8-a44f-5b16-b825-b6478d1f6808" :answer/id #uuid "bfaa76f8-a44f-5b16-b825-b6478d1f6808" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f009da6d-a329-5d22-a54b-3ee89ff22e7c" :answer/id #uuid "f009da6d-a329-5d22-a54b-3ee89ff22e7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6da6ef13-a309-5cf7-9df2-7ba14aab3c75" :question/id #uuid "6da6ef13-a309-5cf7-9df2-7ba14aab3c75" :question/math-academy-id "q-235778" :question/problem "| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{1}{18}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $4$ | $24$ |\n| $6$ | $36$ |\n\nThe table above shows a proportional relationship. Assuming that $y=kx$ for each pair of values, what is the constant of proportionality?\n\nThe constant of proportionality is \n$k=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nTo find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(4,24),$ which corresponds to the second row of the table.\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{4} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the constant of proportionality is $k=6.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-58847121-ba8d-57ee-b1b8-db98957668db"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-58847121-ba8d-57ee-b1b8-db98957668db" :answer-field/id #uuid "58847121-ba8d-57ee-b1b8-db98957668db" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1c08efe0-cd96-5bae-8d3e-479bde90df56" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1c08efe0-cd96-5bae-8d3e-479bde90df56"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c08efe0-cd96-5bae-8d3e-479bde90df56" :answer/id #uuid "1c08efe0-cd96-5bae-8d3e-479bde90df56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152669 :question/problem "Rick is driving on a highway. The relationship between the time $t$ (in minutes) he drives and the distance $d$ (in miles) he covers is shown in the table below.\n\n| Minutes ($t$) | Miles ($d$) |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $3$ |\n| $8$ | $6$ |\n| $12$ | $9$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. There is **not** a proportional relationship between $d$ and $t.$\n\nII. There is a proportional relationship between $d$ and $t.$\n\nIII. The constant of proportionality is $\\frac{3}{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's add a new column to our table and compute the ratio of miles per hour for each row. \n\n| Minutes ($t$) | Miles ($d$) | Miles / Minutes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $8$ | $6$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $12$ | $9$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n\n \n\nThe ratio of miles per minute is the same for each row. Therefore, there is a proportional relationship between $d$ and $t$  and the  constant of proportionality is $k=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186150521 :answer-field/correct 17592186150524 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-07ebc64d-3fda-57ce-9860-9ad032b9cd27" :question/id #uuid "07ebc64d-3fda-57ce-9860-9ad032b9cd27" :question/math-academy-id "q-68218" :question/problem "The relationship between the variables $h$ and $t$ is shown in the following table:\n\n| $t$ | $h$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $8$ |\n| $5$ | $15$ |\n| $6$ | $24$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. There is **not** a proportional relationship between $h$ and $t.$\n\nII. The constant of proportionality is $6.$\n\nIII. There is a proportional relationship between $h$ and $t.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's add a new column to our table and compute the ratio $\\frac{h}{t}:$\n\n| $t$ | $h$ | $\\frac{h}{t}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $8$ | $2$ |\n| $5$ | $15$ | $3$ |\n| $6$ | $24$ | $4$ |\n\nThe ratio $\\frac{h}{t}$ is not the same for each row. Therefore, there is *not* a proportional relationship between $h$ and $t.$ Consequently, there is no constant of proportionality.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7d707df4-4887-57c7-9c58-bad58c0366f6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7d707df4-4887-57c7-9c58-bad58c0366f6" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d707df4-4887-57c7-9c58-bad58c0366f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8aad79c0-c0dc-5134-8e90-9be340bbadb6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-10287edb-ce0a-55d0-a65e-c99f015648c0" "answer-8aad79c0-c0dc-5134-8e90-9be340bbadb6" "answer-8cf82869-6036-577b-92a6-0186bab85966" "answer-972b5786-2f7c-509b-afab-4dc5a8c511d7" "answer-a9bc1b1a-6662-57db-bd16-4ae401d9857d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10287edb-ce0a-55d0-a65e-c99f015648c0" :answer/id #uuid "10287edb-ce0a-55d0-a65e-c99f015648c0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8aad79c0-c0dc-5134-8e90-9be340bbadb6" :answer/id #uuid "8aad79c0-c0dc-5134-8e90-9be340bbadb6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8cf82869-6036-577b-92a6-0186bab85966" :answer/id #uuid "8cf82869-6036-577b-92a6-0186bab85966" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-972b5786-2f7c-509b-afab-4dc5a8c511d7" :answer/id #uuid "972b5786-2f7c-509b-afab-4dc5a8c511d7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9bc1b1a-6662-57db-bd16-4ae401d9857d" :answer/id #uuid "a9bc1b1a-6662-57db-bd16-4ae401d9857d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-93ed9bdb-28a0-5b9b-a66a-358447792b6e" :question/id #uuid "93ed9bdb-28a0-5b9b-a66a-358447792b6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-170863" :question/problem "| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $3$ |\n| $6$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ |\n| $8$ | $6$ |\n\nWhat is the equation for the proportional relationship between $x$ and $y$ shown in the table above? Write the answer in terms of the variable $x.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Every proportional relationship takes the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.  \n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ from the table. Here, let's choose the point $(4,3),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table.\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality by computing the ratio of the $y$-coordinate to the $x$-coordinate, as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{3}{4}x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3e06ce64-71a1-590f-8b42-a5f01b1a4f3b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3e06ce64-71a1-590f-8b42-a5f01b1a4f3b" :answer-field/id #uuid "3e06ce64-71a1-590f-8b42-a5f01b1a4f3b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1e9db7d2-f218-5a54-aeee-3906dbd15695" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1e9db7d2-f218-5a54-aeee-3906dbd15695"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e9db7d2-f218-5a54-aeee-3906dbd15695" :answer/id #uuid "1e9db7d2-f218-5a54-aeee-3906dbd15695" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152522 :question/problem "Elena is painting her fence. There is a proportional relationship between the volume of paint $x$ (in liters) and the area \n $y$ (in square meters) she paints, as shown in the following table:\n\n| Liters of Paint $(x)$ | Square Meters $(y)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $0.12$ | $1$ |\n| $6$ | $50$ |\n| $12$ | $100$ |\n\n \n\nWhat is the equation for the relationship between $x$ and $y?$ Write the answer in terms of the variable $x.$\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nEvery proportional relationship can be written in the form $y=kx,$ where $k$ is the constant of proportionality.\n\nTo compute the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(x,y)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(6,50),$ which corresponds to the second row of the table.\n\nNow, we can find the constant of proportionality as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{50}{6}=\\frac{25}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{25}{3}x.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186149616 :answer-field/correct "answer-58c3c998-6ae7-5db6-bf9a-a1508232dc3a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-58c3c998-6ae7-5db6-bf9a-a1508232dc3a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58c3c998-6ae7-5db6-bf9a-a1508232dc3a" :answer/id #uuid "58c3c998-6ae7-5db6-bf9a-a1508232dc3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25}{3}x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7efe1e34-f652-5c85-ae54-64066cf92fb9" :question/id #uuid "7efe1e34-f652-5c85-ae54-64066cf92fb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-68274" :question/problem "Martha has some bottles, each containing the same amount of water. There is a proportional relationship between the total volume of water $w$ (in ounces) and the number of bottles $b,$ as shown in the table below:     \n\n| Bottles ($b$) | Ounces ($w$) |\n| --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $16$ |\n| $4$ | $32$ |\n| $6$ | $48$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Six bottles contain $45$ ounces of water.\n\nII. The unit rate is $6$ ounces per bottle.\n\nIII. Seven bottles contain $56$ ounces of water." :question/worked-solution "We're told that $w$ and $b$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nTo find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(b,w)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(2,16),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table. \n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{w}{b}=\\frac{16}{2}=8\n$$\n\nThe units of $k$ are \"ounces per bottle\" since we divided the  volume $w$ by the number of bottles $b.$  \n\n Therefore, the relationship between $w$ and $b$ is \n\n$$\nw=8b.\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, with that in mind, let's look at each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The third row of the table corresponds to the point $(6,48).$ This means that $6$ bottles contain a total of $48$ ounces of water, not $45.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We know that $k=8,$ which means that the unit rate is $8$  ounces per bottle.\n\n- Statement III is true.  If we substitute $b=7$ into the relationship $w=8b$ we get \n\n$$\nw=8\\cdot 7=56.\n$$\n\n \nwhich means that $7$ bottles contain a total of $56$ ounces of water.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c5686b1f-667f-5d9e-8a8e-48dc3e903e3d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c5686b1f-667f-5d9e-8a8e-48dc3e903e3d" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5686b1f-667f-5d9e-8a8e-48dc3e903e3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dc21aca6-d7dc-55db-a6cf-bb0561d733e0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-421a6d37-8318-58e7-9d56-7bed4a7abaa3" "answer-f6e23d91-6a29-56e7-82a6-c9e8ad304b64" "answer-5e36da8f-770a-575c-a5c9-5c63a22e2bda" "answer-eef4f183-52b3-5e96-8bad-1a71871c45dc" "answer-dc21aca6-d7dc-55db-a6cf-bb0561d733e0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-421a6d37-8318-58e7-9d56-7bed4a7abaa3" :answer/id #uuid "421a6d37-8318-58e7-9d56-7bed4a7abaa3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6e23d91-6a29-56e7-82a6-c9e8ad304b64" :answer/id #uuid "f6e23d91-6a29-56e7-82a6-c9e8ad304b64" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e36da8f-770a-575c-a5c9-5c63a22e2bda" :answer/id #uuid "5e36da8f-770a-575c-a5c9-5c63a22e2bda" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eef4f183-52b3-5e96-8bad-1a71871c45dc" :answer/id #uuid "eef4f183-52b3-5e96-8bad-1a71871c45dc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc21aca6-d7dc-55db-a6cf-bb0561d733e0" :answer/id #uuid "dc21aca6-d7dc-55db-a6cf-bb0561d733e0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-338f58ff-827c-5814-b929-70f92d28e890" :question/id #uuid "338f58ff-827c-5814-b929-70f92d28e890" :question/math-academy-id "q-91941" :question/problem "There is a proportional relationship between the time $t$ (in seconds) that Jack runs and the distance $d$ (in meters) he covers.\n\n| Time ($t$) | Meters ($d$) |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $7$ |\n| $3$ | $21$ |\n| $5$ | $35$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Jack runs $35$ meters in $5$ seconds.\n\nII. The unit rate is $6$ meters per second.\n\nIII. If Jack runs for $7$ seconds, he covers $49$ meters." :question/worked-solution "We're told that $t$ and $d$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nTo find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(t,d)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(1,7),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table. \n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality as follows:\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{d}{t}=\\frac{7}{1}=7\n$$\n\nThe units of $k$ are \"meters per second\" since we divided the distance $d$ by the time $t.$ Therefore, the relationship between $d$ and $t$ is \n\n$$\nd=7t.\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, with that in mind, let's look at each statement. \n\n- Statement I is true. The third row of the table corresponds to the point $(5,35),$ which means that if Jack runs for $5$ seconds, he covers $35$ meters.\n\n- Statement II is false. We know that $k=7,$ which means that the unit rate is $7$ meters per second.\n\n- Statement III is true. If we substitute $t=7$ into the relationship $d=7t,$ we get\n\n$$\nd=7\\cdot 7=49\n$$\n\nwhich means that If Jack runs for $7$ seconds, he covers $49$ meters.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d070d2b6-c4bd-59d0-bcd9-259eb8cb270c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d070d2b6-c4bd-59d0-bcd9-259eb8cb270c" :answer-field/id #uuid "d070d2b6-c4bd-59d0-bcd9-259eb8cb270c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ca5a5702-e976-50ef-940e-17ce83bfd47f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-27fee0fe-e280-5b8c-bbea-9ea3bb61e749" "answer-ca5a5702-e976-50ef-940e-17ce83bfd47f" "answer-686bcfc3-6a98-58f4-88fb-ff7b50bbc821" "answer-28ab05c6-0cda-5152-aea7-c8a2da8e18fe" "answer-dbaf9052-d1cb-5033-a95f-4c1e5d02dbc2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27fee0fe-e280-5b8c-bbea-9ea3bb61e749" :answer/id #uuid "27fee0fe-e280-5b8c-bbea-9ea3bb61e749" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca5a5702-e976-50ef-940e-17ce83bfd47f" :answer/id #uuid "ca5a5702-e976-50ef-940e-17ce83bfd47f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-686bcfc3-6a98-58f4-88fb-ff7b50bbc821" :answer/id #uuid "686bcfc3-6a98-58f4-88fb-ff7b50bbc821" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28ab05c6-0cda-5152-aea7-c8a2da8e18fe" :answer/id #uuid "28ab05c6-0cda-5152-aea7-c8a2da8e18fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dbaf9052-d1cb-5033-a95f-4c1e5d02dbc2" :answer/id #uuid "dbaf9052-d1cb-5033-a95f-4c1e5d02dbc2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-111207e8-0baf-57b4-8619-90f5c5dcb6bf" :question/id #uuid "111207e8-0baf-57b4-8619-90f5c5dcb6bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-68041" :question/problem "The following table shows a proportional relationship between the weight $w$ (in pounds) of apples purchased at a particular supermarket and the total cost $c$ (in dollars) that customers must pay. \n\n| Weight ($w$) | Cost ($c$) |\n| --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $3$ |\n| $4$ | $6$ |\n| $8$ | $12$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The unit rate is  $$3$ per pound.\n\nII. One pound of apples costs $$1.25.$\n\nIII. The cost of $5$ pounds of apples is $$7.50.$" :question/worked-solution "We're told that $w$ and $c$ have a proportional relationship.\n\nTo find the constant of proportionality, we first need to choose a point $(w,c)$ from the table. Let's choose the point $(2,3),$ which corresponds to the first row of the table.  \n\nWe can find the constant of proportionality as follows: \n\n$$\nk=\\frac{c}{w}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe units of $k$ are \"dollars per pound\" since we divided the cost $c$ by the weight $w.$ \nTherefore, the relationship between $c$ and $w$ is \n\n$$\nc=\\frac{3}{2}w.\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, with that in mind, let's look at each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. We know that $k=\\frac{3}{2},$ which means that the unit rate is $\\frac{3}{2}$ dollars per pound.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $k=\\frac{3}{2}=1.5,$ one pound of apples costs $$1.50.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, if we substitute $w=5$ into the relationship $c=\\frac{3}{2}w,$ we get \n\n$$\nc=\\frac{3}{2}(5)=7.5\n$$\n\nwhich means that the cost of $5$ pounds of apples is $$7.50.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ffd765d1-d231-5044-a1b0-ad0171902d5d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ffd765d1-d231-5044-a1b0-ad0171902d5d" :answer-field/id #uuid "ffd765d1-d231-5044-a1b0-ad0171902d5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5f21a395-c393-5f2a-bcd6-7a3e31353af5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8bd42d55-9989-5adf-9f49-c20d6cabc9ab" "answer-1a081781-6b17-507a-8616-1f8750bb7ead" "answer-cf201e55-6427-539d-970d-9e4f89638821" "answer-9f9c5197-5f75-54fc-ac06-7c6296ca9993" "answer-5f21a395-c393-5f2a-bcd6-7a3e31353af5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8bd42d55-9989-5adf-9f49-c20d6cabc9ab" :answer/id #uuid "8bd42d55-9989-5adf-9f49-c20d6cabc9ab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a081781-6b17-507a-8616-1f8750bb7ead" :answer/id #uuid "1a081781-6b17-507a-8616-1f8750bb7ead" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf201e55-6427-539d-970d-9e4f89638821" :answer/id #uuid "cf201e55-6427-539d-970d-9e4f89638821" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9f9c5197-5f75-54fc-ac06-7c6296ca9993" :answer/id #uuid "9f9c5197-5f75-54fc-ac06-7c6296ca9993" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f21a395-c393-5f2a-bcd6-7a3e31353af5" :answer/id #uuid "5f21a395-c393-5f2a-bcd6-7a3e31353af5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153438 :tutorial/content "Two variables, $x$ and $y,$ are said to have a **proportional relationship** if their ratio is constant. That is, for any $(x,y)$ pair, we have\n\n$$\nk = \\dfrac{y}{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a fixed number called the **constant of proportionality** or **constant of variation.**\n\nAs a concrete example, let's consider the table below. \n\n| $x$ | $y$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ |\n| $4$ | $8$ |\n\nDo $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship?\n\nTo determine whether $x$ and $y$ have a proportional relationship, we first add a new $\\dfrac yx$ column:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $\\dfrac{y}{x}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ |  |\n| $2$ | $4$ |  |\n| $4$ | $8$ |  |\n\nNext, we compute $\\dfrac yx$ for each row:\n\n| $x$ | $y$ | $\\dfrac{y}{x}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2\\div 1 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $4\\div 2 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ |\n| $4$ | $8$ | $8\\div 4 = {\\color{blue}{2}}$ |\n\nSince all the entries in the third column have the same value, this table does indeed represent a proportional relationship. In this case, the constant of proportionality $k = {\\color{blue}{2}}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152311 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152160 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-284c9b33-1577-5c49-9cce-c96ae365ec69"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-284c9b33-1577-5c49-9cce-c96ae365ec69" :question/id #uuid "284c9b33-1577-5c49-9cce-c96ae365ec69" :question/math-academy-id "q-76608" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}x(1-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the section of the polar curve $r=1\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}?$" :question/worked-solution "In polar coordinates, the formula for the line integral is given by\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe connection between polar and Cartesian coordinates gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, along our curve, we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $, where $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8}.$\n\nNow, if $f(x,y)=x(1-{y}^{2})$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta } & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\theta }(1)=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x(1-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3cf0e6db-e5f4-547e-8dad-d9a52bf566a7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3cf0e6db-e5f4-547e-8dad-d9a52bf566a7" :answer-field/id #uuid "3cf0e6db-e5f4-547e-8dad-d9a52bf566a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-97cbc095-95df-53b0-aeb5-aa994a815950" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dc6f0f30-494e-5978-8c42-1f9efcb271d6" "answer-fb5a577e-b9f2-5c3c-87c6-fb5029ee12ce" "answer-3a018c22-1a77-5e85-97e0-1ac9d6577bec" "answer-28f03a0e-89a7-5b16-bf01-1519355c07d9" "answer-97cbc095-95df-53b0-aeb5-aa994a815950"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dc6f0f30-494e-5978-8c42-1f9efcb271d6" :answer/id #uuid "dc6f0f30-494e-5978-8c42-1f9efcb271d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb5a577e-b9f2-5c3c-87c6-fb5029ee12ce" :answer/id #uuid "fb5a577e-b9f2-5c3c-87c6-fb5029ee12ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a018c22-1a77-5e85-97e0-1ac9d6577bec" :answer/id #uuid "3a018c22-1a77-5e85-97e0-1ac9d6577bec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28f03a0e-89a7-5b16-bf01-1519355c07d9" :answer/id #uuid "28f03a0e-89a7-5b16-bf01-1519355c07d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97cbc095-95df-53b0-aeb5-aa994a815950" :answer/id #uuid "97cbc095-95df-53b0-aeb5-aa994a815950" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-f54f5ad8-3bb5-5eec-b65a-dbed1b53f523" :activity/id #uuid "f54f5ad8-3bb5-5eec-b65a-dbed1b53f523" :activity/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Polar Curves" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048694 :activity/course 17592186046660 :activity/steps ["step-bd640546-319a-583f-a987-d6f4f9ca8a1a" "step-a773889b-d594-5730-87d3-566a19243567" "step-7df67029-a10d-5dce-965d-775d1979bb40" "step-61e4b9a7-6a75-55d9-96ed-a67877cbb8d8" "step-167e09bf-7031-5c07-aef8-581424ee1431"] :activity/first-step "step-bd640546-319a-583f-a987-d6f4f9ca8a1a" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-bd640546-319a-583f-a987-d6f4f9ca8a1a" :step/id #uuid "bd640546-319a-583f-a987-d6f4f9ca8a1a" :step/content 17592186152740 :step/next "step-a773889b-d594-5730-87d3-566a19243567" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a773889b-d594-5730-87d3-566a19243567" :step/id #uuid "a773889b-d594-5730-87d3-566a19243567" :step/content "question-284c9b33-1577-5c49-9cce-c96ae365ec69" :step/next "step-7df67029-a10d-5dce-965d-775d1979bb40" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7df67029-a10d-5dce-965d-775d1979bb40" :step/id #uuid "7df67029-a10d-5dce-965d-775d1979bb40" :step/content 17592186345237 :step/next "step-61e4b9a7-6a75-55d9-96ed-a67877cbb8d8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-61e4b9a7-6a75-55d9-96ed-a67877cbb8d8" :step/id #uuid "61e4b9a7-6a75-55d9-96ed-a67877cbb8d8" :step/content 17592186345245 :step/next "step-167e09bf-7031-5c07-aef8-581424ee1431" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-167e09bf-7031-5c07-aef8-581424ee1431" :step/id #uuid "167e09bf-7031-5c07-aef8-581424ee1431" :step/content 17592186284544 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162043 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2c32a996-4944-5e2d-afe2-519907ae493e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-2c32a996-4944-5e2d-afe2-519907ae493e" :question/id #uuid "2c32a996-4944-5e2d-afe2-519907ae493e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140962" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the $3$rd column of the matrix $U?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=2\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the $1$st and $2$nd columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{6}}[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the third column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\\,‖{u}_{3}‖=1.\n$$\n\nSolving this system, which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{3}=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe get the nonzero normalized solution \n\n$$\n{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrix $U:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & 0 & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f80968a1-b5d3-5a74-a9ab-07fd72693189"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f80968a1-b5d3-5a74-a9ab-07fd72693189" :answer-field/id #uuid "f80968a1-b5d3-5a74-a9ab-07fd72693189" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-fie